module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8692', KITS: {"3.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.1.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Theme 1 covers identity and relationships, healthy living, and education and work — the three areas of your own life you are most often asked to describe, compare and give opinions on. Talking about people needs adjectives that agree and comparisons; talking about habits needs frequency phrases and desde hace; talking about school and the future needs impersonal rules and si + present + future. Getting these structures secure lets you say what you actually think, not just recite set phrases.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Identity and relationships with others","t":"This topic covers describing yourself and the people close to you: physical description, personality, and family and friendship relationships. Build description from three layers — who someone is, what they are like, and why the relationship matters to you: Mi mejor amiga se llama Laura; es muy graciosa y siempre me hace reír (My best friend is called Laura; she is very funny and always makes me laugh). Personality adjectives agree like any other adjective: gracioso/graciosa, simpático/simpática, trabajador/trabajadora. Comparisons are common in this topic: Mi hermano es más alto que yo, pero yo soy más paciente que él (My brother is taller than me, but I am more patient than him). The specification notes that you are expected to respond according to your own interests and experiences, so prepare true, specific detail about your own family and friends rather than generic sentences."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Healthy living and lifestyle","t":"This topic covers diet, exercise, sleep and habits, both good and bad, and giving opinions with reasons about them. State a habit, then evaluate it: Como muchas verduras y hago deporte tres veces por semana, así que llevo una vida bastante sana (I eat a lot of vegetables and do sport three times a week, so I lead quite a healthy life). Negative habits use the same structures: Algunos jóvenes fuman o comen demasiada comida basura, lo cual es malo para la salud (Some young people smoke or eat too much junk food, which is bad for their health). desde hace + a time period describes something continuing from the past into the present: Hago yoga desde hace dos años (I have been doing yoga for two years). Reflexive verbs are common here too: acostarse, despertarse, cuidarse (to go to bed, to wake up, to look after oneself). You are expected to give a genuine opinion with a reason whenever this topic comes up, not just describe a habit."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"Education and work","t":"This topic covers school subjects and opinions of them, rules and pressures at school, and part-time jobs or future career plans. Give a subject opinion with a reason: Prefiero las ciencias porque el profesor explica todo muy claramente (I prefer science because the teacher explains everything very clearly). School rules use hay que or se debe: Hay que llevar uniforme y no se puede usar el móvil en clase (You have to wear uniform and you cannot use your phone in class). Future plans typically combine the simple future or ir a + infinitive with a conditional structure built on si: Si apruebo mis exámenes, estudiaré en la universidad; si no, buscaré un trabajo a tiempo parcial (If I pass my exams, I will study at university; if not, I will look for a part-time job) — si with the present tense pairs with the future tense in the result clause, not the conditional. You are expected to give a genuine opinion with a reason about school and work, drawing on your own experience rather than a generic answer."}],"examples":[{"title":"Describing a friend using comparison","setup":"Translate into Spanish (the speaker is male): 'My friend Marta is more sociable than me, but I am funnier than her.'","steps":["'More sociable than me' uses más + adjective + que: más sociable que yo.","sociable does not change for gender, so it stays the same form for Marta.","'Funnier than her' follows the same pattern: más gracioso + que ella — gracioso agrees with the male speaker.","Full sentence: Mi amiga Marta es más sociable que yo, pero yo soy más gracioso que ella."],"cues":["Which comparison structure means 'more … than'?","Does sociable change its ending for a feminine subject?","Which pronoun follows que when comparing to 'her'?","Write the complete sentence with both comparisons."]},{"title":"Translating a healthy-living text","setup":"Translate into English: 'Como mucha fruta y verdura, y también hago ejercicio cada mañana antes de ir al colegio. Sin embargo, no duermo lo suficiente entre semana porque tengo muchos deberes.'","steps":["The first sentence has two linked habits: como mucha fruta y verdura (I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables) and hago ejercicio cada mañana antes de ir al colegio (I do exercise every morning before going to school).","sin embargo signals a contrast: 'however'.","no duermo lo suficiente entre semana translates as 'I don't sleep enough during the week'.","Full English answer: I eat a lot of fruit and vegetables, and I also exercise every morning before going to school. However, I don't sleep enough during the week because I have a lot of homework."],"cues":["What two healthy habits are named in the first sentence?","What does sin embargo signal is coming next?","What is the writer's problem with sleep?","Give the complete English translation of both sentences."]},{"title":"School rules with hay que and se puede","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'At my school you have to arrive on time, and you cannot eat in class.'","steps":["'You have to' is the impersonal hay que + infinitive: hay que llegar.","'on time' is the set phrase a la hora, not a word-for-word 'en tiempo': hay que llegar a la hora.","'You cannot' is no se puede + infinitive: no se puede comer en clase.","Full sentence, with the place phrase first: En mi colegio hay que llegar a la hora, y no se puede comer en clase."],"cues":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to', and what verb form follows it?","Is 'on time' translated word for word, or with a set phrase?","Which impersonal phrase means 'you cannot'?","Where does 'at my school' go, and how are the two halves joined?"]},{"title":"Future plans with si + present + future","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'If I pass my exams, I will study medicine; if not, I will look for a job.'","steps":["'If I pass' uses si with the present tense: si apruebo mis exámenes.","The result clause takes the simple future, not the conditional: estudiaré medicina.","'If not' is si no, and its result also takes the simple future: buscaré un trabajo.","Full sentence: Si apruebo mis exámenes, estudiaré medicina; si no, buscaré un trabajo."],"cues":["Which tense follows si here — present, or future?","Which tense goes in the result clause after that si clause?","How do you say 'if not', and what tense follows it?","Write the complete sentence, both halves joined correctly."]}],"check":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'more … than' and 'less … than'.","a":"más … que; menos … que."},{"q":"What does desde hace + a time period express?","a":"Something that has been continuing from the past up to now — 'I have been doing X for [time period]'."},{"q":"Which tense goes in the result clause after si + present tense ('if I do X, I will do Y')?","a":"The simple future, not the conditional."},{"q":"Give the Spanish impersonal phrase for 'you cannot' plus an infinitive.","a":"No se puede + infinitive."}]},"room":{"facts":["Personality and description adjectives agree with the person described: gracioso/graciosa, trabajador/trabajadora, simpático/simpática.","Comparison: más/menos + adjective + que (more/less … than); tan + adjective + como (as … as).","desde hace + time period = 'have been doing X for [time]', with the verb in the present tense.","School rules: hay que / se debe + infinitive (you have to); no se puede + infinitive (you cannot).","si + present tense pairs with the future tense in the result clause: Si apruebo, estudiaré (If I pass, I will study) — never si + present with a conditional result."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into English: 'Mi hermana es más trabajadora que yo.'","a":"My sister is harder-working / more hard-working than me.","sol":"My sister is harder-working than me. más trabajadora que yo is the comparative 'more … than' structure, with trabajadora agreeing with hermana (feminine).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["más ... que is a comparison — which way does it point, more or less?","The adjective is built from a verb you know — what personal quality does that give?","Give the complete English sentence, keeping the comparative meaning."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I eat a lot of vegetables every day.'","a":"Como muchas verduras todos los días.","sol":"Como muchas verduras todos los días. como is the yo form of comer; muchas agrees with verduras (feminine plural).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What is the yo form of comer?","Check the gender and number of the noun before choosing the ending of the quantity word.","Verb, then the quantity word agreeing with its noun, then the time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'You have to wear uniform at my school.'","a":"Hay que llevar uniforme en mi colegio.","sol":"Hay que llevar uniforme en mi colegio. hay que + infinitive is the impersonal 'you have to'; the phrase does not change for who is being addressed.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase means 'you have to'?","What form does the verb after hay que take?","Impersonal phrase, infinitive, object, then the place."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Give the Spanish for: 'I have been learning Spanish for three years.'","a":"Aprendo español desde hace tres años.","sol":"Aprendo español desde hace tres años. desde hace + a time period, with the verb in the present tense (aprendo), expresses an action continuing up to now.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which tense does the verb take with desde hace, present or past?","Where does desde hace go in the sentence?","Present-tense verb, object, then the time expression at the end."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Mis padres son muy estrictos: no me dejan salir entre semana y tengo que hacer mis deberes antes de cenar. Sin embargo, los fines de semana soy bastante libre.' (a) Give one thing the writer's parents do not allow. (b) What is different at the weekend?","a":"(a) They don't let the writer go out during the week. (b) The writer is fairly free (has more freedom) at the weekend.","sol":"(a) No me dejan salir entre semana — they don't let me go out during the week. (b) los fines de semana soy bastante libre — at the weekends I am fairly free. sin embargo (however) signals the contrast between the strict weekday rules and the freer weekend.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the sentence before sin embargo for what is not allowed.","sin embargo signals a change — read what follows it for the second answer.","Two short English answers: (a) one thing that is forbidden, (b) how the weekend differs."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Although I don't like exercise, I know it's important for my health.'","a":"Aunque no me gusta el ejercicio, sé que es importante para mi salud.","sol":"Aunque no me gusta el ejercicio, sé que es importante para mi salud. aunque (although) introduces the contrast; sé is the irregular yo form of saber, followed by que and a clause.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which word means 'although'?","Which verb means 'to know [a fact]', and is it regular in the yo form?","Write the complete sentence, keeping both clauses linked by aunque."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'In my opinion, homework is useful, but sometimes we have too much.'","a":"En mi opinión, los deberes son útiles, pero a veces tenemos demasiados.","sol":"En mi opinión, los deberes son útiles, pero a veces tenemos demasiados. deberes is plural, so son and útiles both agree; demasiados agrees with deberes (masculine plural) even though the noun itself is not repeated.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Is deberes singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb and adjective?","'too much' here stands in for the noun just mentioned — make the quantity word agree with that noun.","Opinion phrase, subject + verb + adjective, then the contrast word, then verb + quantity word."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'El año que viene, si consigo buenas notas, iré a la universidad para estudiar medicina. Si no, buscaré un aprendizaje en un hospital, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente.' Explain both of the writer's possible plans and the reason given for the second one.","a":"If they get good grades, they will go to university to study medicine; if not, they will look for an apprenticeship in a hospital, because they have always been interested in helping people.","sol":"El año que viene, si consigo buenas notas, iré a la universidad para estudiar medicina — next year, if I get good grades, I will go to university to study medicine. Si no, buscaré un aprendizaje en un hospital — if not, I will look for an apprenticeship in a hospital, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente — because I have always been interested in helping people.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The text gives two different possible futures, joined by si and si no.","Find the reason clause at the very end, introduced by porque.","Give both plans and the reason, all in English."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My grandmother is as generous as she is patient, and she has looked after me since I was a child.'","a":"Mi abuela es tan generosa como paciente, y me ha cuidado desde que yo era pequeño/a.","sol":"Mi abuela es tan generosa como paciente, y me ha cuidado desde que yo era pequeño/a. tan…como makes the equal comparison between two adjectives describing the same person; me ha cuidado is the present perfect of cuidar with me as a direct object pronoun ('has looked after me'); desde que + a past tense clause (era) gives 'since [a point in the past]', different from desde hace + a time period. Adding yo before era makes clear that it is the speaker, not the grandmother, who was a child.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which comparison structure joins two adjectives about the same person as equally true?","What tense is 'has looked after', and which pronoun does it need?","One sentence: the comparison clause, then y, then the perfect-tense clause ending with a 'since' clause that has its own verb and subject."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about your lifestyle and future plans, covering: (1) a healthy or unhealthy habit and your opinion of it, (2) a school rule you have to follow, (3) what you will do if you pass your exams and what you will do if you do not.","a":"A paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish, covering all three bullet points with an opinion, a school rule using hay que or se puede, and a si + present + future structure for both possible outcomes.","sol":"Llevo una vida bastante sana porque hago deporte tres veces por semana y como mucha fruta, aunque a veces como demasiada comida basura los fines de semana. En mi colegio hay que llevar uniforme y no se puede usar el móvil en clase; a veces me parece estricto, pero entiendo las reglas. Si apruebo mis exámenes este verano, estudiaré ciencias y luego iré a la universidad. Si no consigo las notas que necesito, buscaré un aprendizaje en la sanidad, porque siempre me ha interesado ayudar a la gente. (88 words: habit + opinion, a school rule, and both si + present + future outcomes.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures from this room express a habit, an obligation and a condition?","The school-rule bullet needs an impersonal structure.","The future bullet needs two linked clauses, one for each possible outcome."],"codes":["Topic 1","Topic 2","Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Even though my brother and I argue sometimes, we get on well in general, and I know I can always count on him.'","a":"Aunque mi hermano y yo discutimos a veces, nos llevamos bien en general, y sé que siempre puedo contar con él.","sol":"Aunque mi hermano y yo discutimos a veces, nos llevamos bien en general, y sé que siempre puedo contar con él. nos llevamos bien is the reflexive-style expression for 'we get on well'; contar con is 'to count/rely on', taking con before the pronoun él.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which reflexive expression means 'to get on well' with someone?","'Count on' needs a specific preposition before the pronoun — which one?","Write the complete sentence with all three clauses."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I try to sleep enough during the week, but sometimes I go to bed very late because I have too much homework.'","a":"Intento dormir lo suficiente entre semana, pero a veces me acuesto muy tarde porque tengo demasiados deberes.","sol":"Intento dormir lo suficiente entre semana, pero a veces me acuesto muy tarde porque tengo demasiados deberes. intentar + infinitive gives 'to try to'; me acuesto is the reflexive acostarse (o→ue), 'I go to bed'; demasiados agrees with deberes (masculine plural).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which verb means 'to try to' + another verb?","Which reflexive verb means 'to go to bed', and does its stem change?","One complete sentence with two linked clauses, joined by pero."],"codes":["Topic 2","Topic 3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'as … as' (equal comparison).","a":"tan … como"},{"q":"Which tense goes in the result clause after si + present tense?","a":"The simple future."},{"q":"Translate: 'no se puede usar el móvil en clase'.","a":"You cannot use your phone in class."},{"q":"Give the Spanish reflexive expression for 'to get on well' (with someone).","a":"llevarse bien (con alguien)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"gracioso/a, trabajador(a), simpático/a","back":"funny, hard-working, nice/kind (agree with the person). Mi amigo es muy gracioso. (My friend is very funny.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"más/menos ... que","back":"more/less ... than. Es más alto que yo. (He is taller than me.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"llevarse bien / mal (con alguien)","back":"to get on well / badly (with someone). Me llevo bien con mi hermana. (I get on well with my sister.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"contar con alguien","back":"to count/rely on someone. Puedo contar con mis amigos. (I can count on my friends.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"llevar una vida sana","back":"to lead a healthy life. Llevo una vida bastante sana. (I lead quite a healthy life.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"hacer deporte / hacer ejercicio","back":"to do sport / to exercise. Hago deporte tres veces por semana. (I do sport three times a week.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"desde hace + tiempo","back":"for [time period] (continuing to now). Hago yoga desde hace dos años. (I have been doing yoga for two years.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"la comida basura","back":"junk food. Como demasiada comida basura. (I eat too much junk food.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"hay que / se debe + infinitivo","back":"you have to. Hay que llegar a la hora. (You have to arrive on time.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"no se puede + infinitivo","back":"you cannot. No se puede comer en clase. (You cannot eat in class.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"un aprendizaje","back":"an apprenticeship. Buscaré un aprendizaje. (I will look for an apprenticeship.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"si + presente..., + futuro","back":"if + present..., + future (not conditional). Si apruebo, estudiaré medicina. (If I pass, I will study medicine.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: identidad y relaciones","items":["gracioso/a - funny","trabajador(a) - hard-working","simpático/a - nice, kind","generoso/a - generous","paciente - patient","llevarse bien / mal (con) - to get on well / badly (with)","contar con alguien - to count on someone","el mejor amigo / la mejor amiga - best friend","discutir - to argue","cuidar (a alguien) - to look after (someone)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: vida sana","items":["llevar una vida sana - to lead a healthy life","hacer deporte / ejercicio - to do sport / exercise","la comida basura - junk food","las verduras / la fruta - vegetables / fruit","dormir lo suficiente - to sleep enough","acostarse / despertarse - to go to bed / to wake up","cuidarse - to look after oneself"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: educación y trabajo","items":["el uniforme - uniform","los deberes - homework","un aprendizaje - an apprenticeship","aprobar (un examen) - to pass (an exam)","la sanidad - healthcare","estricto/a - strict","las ciencias - science","a tiempo parcial - part-time"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: comparaciones, hay que y el futuro con si","items":["más/menos + adjetivo + que: Es más alto que yo. (He is taller than me.)","tan + adjetivo + como: Es tan paciente como generosa. (She is as patient as she is generous.)","desde hace + tiempo (presente): Vivo aquí desde hace cinco años. (I have lived here for five years.)","desde que + cláusula (pasado): Me cuida desde que era niño. (She has looked after me since I was a child.)","hay que / se debe + infinitivo: Hay que estudiar más. (You have to study more.)","no se puede + infinitivo: No se puede fumar aquí. (You cannot smoke here.)","si + presente, + futuro: Si trabajo mucho, aprobaré. (If I work hard, I will pass.)","el futuro simple, formado sobre el infinitivo (ser e ir incluidos): estudiaré, buscaré, iré, seré. Si apruebo, iré a la universidad. (If I pass, I'll go to university.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.538Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solved correctly and the Spanish throughout is accurate, so this is close to publishable; fix the missing second vocabulary field per topic, the misleading 'a la hora' claim in worked example 3, and add accepted alternatives to the desde hace question before release."}}},"3.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.1.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Popular culture is one of the topics you can expect to talk and write about most naturally, because it covers what you actually do and enjoy: free-time activities, festivals and celebrations, and celebrity culture. It rewards genuine opinions with reasons rather than lists of facts, so this room builds the vocabulary and structures — frequency expressions, gustar-type verbs, and near-future plans — that let you describe your own interests fluently and accurately.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Free-time activities","t":"This topic covers what you do in your free time and how often you do it. gustar-type verbs agree with what is liked, not with the person: me gusta el fútbol (singular thing liked) but me gustan los videojuegos (plural things liked); other verbs follow the same pattern — me encanta, me interesa, me aburre. Frequency adverbs and phrases are essential here: siempre, normalmente, a veces, raras veces, nunca, todos los fines de semana. soler + infinitive expresses a habitual action: Suelo jugar al baloncesto los sábados (I usually play basketball on Saturdays). Near-future plans use ir a + infinitive: Voy a ver una película esta noche (I am going to watch a film tonight)."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Customs, festivals and celebrations","t":"This topic covers how festivals and celebrations are marked in Spanish-speaking countries and in your own culture, and comparing the two. Describe what happens using se + verb for a general custom: Se celebra con fuegos artificiales y música en la calle (It is celebrated with fireworks and music in the street). Past celebrations use the preterite for completed actions: El año pasado fuimos a un desfile y comimos platos tradicionales (Last year we went to a parade and ate traditional dishes). Comparing customs uses the same comparison structures as elsewhere: En mi país celebramos de una manera diferente / parecida (In my country we celebrate in a different / similar way)."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"Celebrity culture","t":"This topic covers fame, role models, and the influence of celebrities and social media influencers, including opinions on that influence. Give an opinion with a reason: Admiro a esta cantante porque usa su fama para ayudar a causas benéficas (I admire this singer because she uses her fame to help charitable causes). Describing a celebrity's career or plans often uses the near future or the present perfect: Ha ganado varios premios y va a lanzar un álbum nuevo (She has won several awards and is going to release a new album). Critical opinions about celebrity culture are just as valid as admiring ones: Algunos famosos son buenos modelos a seguir, pero otros solo buscan atención (Some celebrities are good role models, but others only seek attention)."}],"examples":[{"title":"gustar-type verbs agreeing with a plural thing liked","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I love video games, but board games interest me more.'","steps":["'I love' with a plural thing liked (video games) uses me encantan, not me encanta.","'video games' is los videojuegos.","'board games interest me more' also has a plural subject, so interesar agrees: me interesan más los juegos de mesa.","Full sentence: Me encantan los videojuegos, pero me interesan más los juegos de mesa."],"cues":["Is the thing liked singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb ending?","How do you say 'video games' in Spanish?","Which verb form agrees with 'board games' in the second clause?","What is the complete sentence, with both clauses joined by pero?"]},{"title":"Translation into English: a festival description","setup":"Translate into English: 'En muchos pueblos de España, la Semana Santa se celebra con procesiones solemnes por las calles, y mucha gente lleva trajes tradicionales.'","steps":["En muchos pueblos de España translates as 'In many towns/villages in Spain'.","se celebra con procesiones solemnes por las calles translates as 'is celebrated with solemn processions through the streets' — se + verb describes the general custom, with no specific subject named.","mucha gente lleva trajes tradicionales translates as 'many people wear traditional costumes'.","Full English answer: In many towns in Spain, Holy Week is celebrated with solemn processions through the streets, and many people wear traditional costumes."],"cues":["Where does the text say this custom takes place?","What structure is se celebra?","What do many people wear, according to the text?","What is the complete English translation?"]},{"title":"Describing a celebrity's achievements and plans","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'This footballer has scored fifty goals this season, and he is going to sign for a new club next year.'","steps":["'has scored' is the present perfect: ha marcado.","'This footballer' is the subject: este futbolista; 'fifty goals this season' follows the verb: cincuenta goles esta temporada.","'is going to sign' uses the near future ir a + infinitive: va a fichar.","Full sentence: Este futbolista ha marcado cincuenta goles esta temporada, y va a fichar por un nuevo club el año que viene."],"cues":["Which tense is 'has scored', and how is it formed in Spanish?","How do you say 'this footballer', and what comes after the verb?","Which structure expresses a future plan here?","What is the complete sentence, with both verb forms?"]},{"title":"Adjective agreement with a plural subject","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'These documentaries about music are really interesting.'","steps":["'These documentaries' is plural: estos documentales.","interesante has only one form for masculine and feminine, but it still adds -s to agree with a plural noun: interesantes.","'about music' follows the noun: sobre música.","Full sentence: Estos documentales sobre música son muy interesantes."],"cues":["Is 'these documentaries' singular or plural, and what article/demonstrative fits that?","Does interesante change for gender? Does it change for number?","Where does 'about music' go in the sentence?","What is the complete sentence, with the adjective in its correct form?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How does gustar (and encantar, interesar) agree — with the person, or with the thing liked?","a":"With the thing liked: me gusta (singular), me gustan (plural)."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'I usually' + an activity, using soler.","a":"Suelo + infinitive, e.g. Suelo jugar al fútbol."},{"q":"Which structure describes a general custom with no specific subject named, such as 'it is celebrated with...'?","a":"se + verb, e.g. se celebra con..."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'is going to' + a verb, for a near-future plan.","a":"va a + infinitive."}]},"room":{"facts":["gustar-type verbs (gustar, encantar, interesar, aburrir) agree with the thing liked, not the person: me gusta/me gustan.","Frequency: siempre, normalmente, a veces, raras veces, nunca; soler + infinitive for a habitual action.","se + verb describes a general custom with no specific subject: se celebra, se come, se organiza.","The near future (ir a + infinitive) and the present perfect (haber + past participle) are both common for describing celebrities' plans and achievements.","Adjectives ending in -e (interesante, importante) do not change for gender but do add -s for a plural noun."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I love football.'","a":"Me encanta el fútbol.","sol":"Me encanta el fútbol. fútbol is singular, so encantar takes its singular form, encanta.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Is 'football' singular or plural?","Which form of encantar matches a singular thing liked?","Pronoun + verb + the sport with its article."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I usually watch series on the weekend.'","a":"Suelo ver series el fin de semana.","sol":"Suelo ver series el fin de semana. soler is a stem-changing verb (o→ue) followed directly by the infinitive ver.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which structure expresses 'usually' doing something, changing its stem in the yo form?","What follows soler directly?","Habit verb in the yo form + infinitive + time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Fireworks are set off at midnight.'","a":"Se lanzan fuegos artificiales a medianoche.","sol":"Se lanzan fuegos artificiales a medianoche. se + verb (lanzan, agreeing with the plural fuegos artificiales) describes the custom without naming who does it.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["No specific person is named as doing this — which structure fits that?","Does the verb agree with a singular or plural noun here?","Impersonal pronoun + plural verb + noun + time phrase."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This singer is going to release a new song.'","a":"Esta cantante va a lanzar una nueva canción.","sol":"Esta cantante va a lanzar una nueva canción. va a + infinitive is the near future; lanzar (to release/launch) collocates naturally with canción or álbum. (Este cantante is equally acceptable, since 'this singer' is ungendered in English.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which structure expresses a near-future plan?","What verb naturally goes with 'release a song' in Spanish?","Subject + va a + infinitive + object."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Suelo escuchar música mientras hago mis deberes, pero raras veces veo la televisión entre semana.'","a":"I usually listen to music while I do my homework, but I rarely watch television during the week.","sol":"Suelo escuchar música mientras hago mis deberes, pero raras veces veo la televisión entre semana — I usually listen to music while I do my homework, but I rarely watch television during the week.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure translates 'usually' here?","What does raras veces mean?","Give one complete English sentence, keeping both halves linked."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Los fines de semana suelo quedar con mis amigos para ver una película o jugar a videojuegos en línea. Raras veces salgo entre semana porque tengo muchos deberes.' What does the writer usually do at weekends, and how often do they go out during the week?","a":"They usually meet friends to watch a film or play video games online; they rarely go out during the week (because of homework).","sol":"Los fines de semana suelo quedar con mis amigos para ver una película o jugar a videojuegos en línea — at weekends I usually meet up with my friends to watch a film or play video games online. Raras veces salgo entre semana porque tengo muchos deberes — I rarely go out during the week because I have a lot of homework.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the weekend activities named in the first sentence — there are two.","raras veces is a frequency phrase — what does it mean?","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'In my country, we don't celebrate this festival, but we have similar traditions at Christmas.'","a":"En mi país, no celebramos este festival, pero tenemos tradiciones parecidas en Navidad.","sol":"En mi país, no celebramos este festival, pero tenemos tradiciones parecidas en Navidad. celebramos and tenemos are both nosotros forms, since the writer is speaking about their own country's customs generally; parecidas agrees with tradiciones (feminine plural).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Who is speaking about their own country's customs — which verb ending fits that?","Check the agreement of 'similar' with the noun it describes.","One complete sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Many young people follow influencers on social media because they find their content entertaining.'","a":"Muchos jóvenes siguen a influencers en las redes sociales porque les parece entretenido su contenido.","sol":"Muchos jóvenes siguen a influencers/personas influyentes en las redes sociales porque les parece entretenido su contenido. seguir is irregular (e→i) in some forms; parecer works like gustar, agreeing with contenido (singular) and taking les for 'they find it'. personas influyentes is an accepted alternative to influencers.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which verb for 'to follow' is stem-changing in some forms?","Which interesar-type verb expresses 'they find [something] entertaining'?","One complete sentence with a reason clause."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Aunque algunos famosos usan su plataforma para promover causas importantes, a otros solo les interesa la fama y el dinero, lo cual me parece una lástima.' What contrast does the writer draw, and what is their opinion of the second group?","a":"Some celebrities use their platform for important causes, while others are only interested in fame and money — the writer thinks that is a shame.","sol":"Aunque algunos famosos usan su plataforma para promover causas importantes — although some celebrities use their platform to promote important causes — a otros solo les interesa la fama y el dinero — others are only interested in fame and money — lo cual me parece una lástima — which the writer thinks is a shame.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["aunque signals a contrast between two groups — find both.","The second group is described by what interests them, using an interesar-type structure.","Give the contrast and the writer's opinion, in English."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish comparing a festival or celebration in a Spanish-speaking country with one in your own country, covering: (1) how the Spanish-speaking festival is celebrated, (2) one similarity or difference with a festival you know, (3) your opinion of it.","a":"e.g. Day of the Dead in Mexico: altars and flowers to remember relatives; different from my country, where we don't celebrate the dead this way; a beautiful custom that shows respect for family, which I would like to see one day.","sol":"En México, el Día de Muertos se celebra con altares coloridos y flores de cempasúchil para recordar a los familiares fallecidos. El año pasado vi un desfile en un documental, y las familias preparaban la comida favorita de sus seres queridos y visitaban el cementerio. Es bastante diferente de las tradiciones de mi país, donde no solemos celebrar a los muertos de esta manera. Me parece una costumbre preciosa porque muestra mucho respeto hacia la familia, y algún día me gustaría viajar a México para verlo en persona. (88 words: se celebra for the custom, es diferente de for the comparison, me parece for the opinion; vi/preparaban give a past reference and me gustaría viajar a future one.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which structure describes how a custom is carried out without naming who does it?","Which words compare one tradition with another, and which phrase gives an opinion?","Three short sections in the order of the bullets, about 90 words in all."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Although I don't usually follow celebrity news, I admire people who use their fame to help others, because it shows their success means more than just money.'","a":"Aunque no suelo seguir las noticias sobre famosos, admiro a las personas que usan su fama para ayudar a otros, porque demuestra que su éxito significa más que solo dinero.","sol":"Aunque no suelo seguir las noticias sobre famosos, admiro a las personas que usan su fama para ayudar a otros, porque demuestra que su éxito significa más que solo dinero. soler + infinitive in the negative gives 'don't usually'; a las personas needs the personal a since personas is a direct object referring to people; más que + a noun (not más de) compares two different things.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["Where does the negative no go relative to soler and the infinitive it introduces?","'admire people' needs a small extra word before a direct object that refers to people.","One sentence: a concession clause, then a main clause with a relative clause about the people, then a reason clause."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Last year we went to a traditional festival in a small village, where they organised a parade and everyone danced in the street until midnight.'","a":"El año pasado fuimos a un festival tradicional en un pueblo pequeño, donde organizaron un desfile y todo el mundo bailó en la calle hasta la medianoche.","sol":"El año pasado fuimos a un festival tradicional en un pueblo pequeño, donde organizaron un desfile y todo el mundo bailó en la calle hasta la medianoche. fuimos (ir) and bailó are both preterite for completed past actions; organizaron uses the third-person plural (they, impersonal) rather than se + verb, both valid ways to describe an organised custom.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["Which past tense is needed for completed one-off actions like 'went' and 'danced'?","'they organised' can use the plain third-person plural, without se — check the verb ending.","Write the complete sentence, including the relative clause starting with donde."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This actor has won an award for his new film, and many young people consider him a good role model.'","a":"Este actor ha ganado un premio por su nueva película, y muchos jóvenes lo consideran un buen modelo a seguir.","sol":"Este actor ha ganado un premio por su nueva película, y muchos jóvenes lo consideran un buen modelo a seguir. ha ganado is the present perfect; lo consideran uses the direct object pronoun lo (him) before the verb, with consideran agreeing with muchos jóvenes.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which tense is 'has won', and how is it formed?","'Consider him' needs a direct object pronoun before the verb — which one?","One complete sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"You are describing a photo to your teacher. The photo shows a family sitting round a table with a birthday cake and balloons. Answer in Spanish, giving two details: ¿Qué hay en la foto?","a":"e.g. En la foto hay una familia en la mesa; celebran un cumpleaños y hay una tarta.","sol":"Any two correct details in Spanish: hay una familia / están sentados en la mesa / hay una tarta (de cumpleaños) / hay globos / celebran un cumpleaños. Use hay + noun for what is there and a present-tense verb for what the people are doing.","hints":["Which verb says what there is in a picture?","Name the people first, then an object or the action.","Two short Spanish sentences, or one sentence with two details joined by y."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"You are talking to a Spanish friend about free time. Say in Spanish: (1) what you usually do at the weekend; (2) ask what your friend likes watching on TV.","a":"e.g. Los fines de semana suelo salir con mis amigos. ¿Qué te gusta ver en la televisión?","sol":"(1) needs a habit: suelo + infinitive, or a frequency word with a present-tense verb, plus el fin de semana / los fines de semana. (2) needs a question opening with ¿Qué…? and the tú form of the liking structure, because you are talking to a friend.","hints":["Part 1 is a habit; part 2 is a question to a friend you address as tú.","Which structure states what you usually do, and how do you ask a friend what they like?","One statement, then one question with both question marks."],"m":4,"d":2,"codes":["Topic 1"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'to usually do something', using soler.","a":"soler + infinitive"},{"q":"Translate: 'me interesan los documentales'.","a":"documentaries interest me"},{"q":"Which tense is used for a completed one-off action in the past, such as 'we went' or 'they danced'?","a":"The preterite."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'is going to release' (an album, a song).","a":"va a lanzar"}]},"cards":[{"front":"me gusta(n) / me encanta(n)","back":"I like / I love (agrees with the thing liked). Me encantan los videojuegos. (I love video games.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"soler + infinitivo","back":"to usually do something. Suelo jugar al baloncesto. (I usually play basketball.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"a veces / raras veces / nunca","back":"sometimes / rarely / never. Raras veces salgo entre semana. (I rarely go out during the week.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"los juegos de mesa","back":"board games. Me interesan más los juegos de mesa. (Board games interest me more.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"se celebra / se organiza","back":"it is celebrated / it is organised. Se celebra con fuegos artificiales. (It is celebrated with fireworks.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"un desfile","back":"a parade. Fuimos a un desfile. (We went to a parade.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"los fuegos artificiales","back":"fireworks. Se lanzan fuegos artificiales a medianoche. (Fireworks are set off at midnight.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"parecido/a a / diferente de","back":"similar to / different from. Es bastante diferente de mi cultura. (It's quite different from my culture.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"un modelo a seguir","back":"a role model. Es un buen modelo a seguir. (He/She is a good role model.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"las redes sociales","back":"social media. Muchos jóvenes usan las redes sociales. (Many young people use social media.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"va a lanzar (un álbum) / va a hacer (una gira)","back":"is going to release (an album) / go on (a tour). Va a lanzar un álbum nuevo y va a hacer una gira mundial. (She is going to release a new album and go on a world tour.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"la fama","back":"fame. Usa su fama para ayudar a otros. (He/She uses their fame to help others.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: actividades de ocio","items":["jugar a videojuegos - to play video games","ver una película / una serie - to watch a film / a series","leer una novela - to read a novel","quedar con amigos - to meet up with friends","hacer deporte - to do sport","escuchar música - to listen to music","los juegos de mesa - board games","un documental - a documentary","en línea - online","el fin de semana - the weekend"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: fiestas y celebraciones","items":["un desfile - a parade","los fuegos artificiales - fireworks","un traje tradicional - a traditional costume/outfit","celebrar / la celebración - to celebrate / celebration","la Navidad - Christmas","una fiesta - a festival, celebration","parecido/a - similar","diferente - different"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: la fama y las redes sociales","items":["un famoso / una famosa - a celebrity","las redes sociales - social media","un modelo a seguir - a role model","la fama - fame","un premio - a prize, award","seguir (a alguien) - to follow (someone)","un influencer / una persona influyente - an influencer","el contenido - content"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: gustar, se + verbo y el futuro próximo","items":["gustar/encantar/interesar/aburrir concuerdan con lo que gusta, no con la persona: Me gusta el cine. Me gustan los conciertos.","soler + infinitivo (o→ue): Suelo salir los viernes. (I usually go out on Fridays.)","se + verbo para una costumbre general: Se come mucho marisco en esta fiesta. (A lot of seafood is eaten at this festival.)","el futuro próximo: ir a + infinitivo: Va a hacer una gira mundial. (He/She is going to go on a world tour.)","el pretérito perfecto: haber + participio: Ha ganado varios premios. (She has won several awards.)","el pretérito para acciones pasadas y terminadas: Fuimos a un desfile y bailamos toda la noche. (We went to a parade and danced all night.)","adjetivos terminados en -e no cambian de género pero sí de número: interesante → interesantes."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.538Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the Spanish is idiomatic throughout; the fixes needed are cosmetic — put the 90-word task's answer key in Spanish, soften three hints that name the structure being credited, and align the translation mark allocations with the specification's tick-grid."}}},"3.1.3":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.1.3","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"This theme covers travel and tourism, media and technology, and the environment and where people live — three areas where you need both descriptive vocabulary and opinion language, since exam tasks consistently ask not just what you do but what you think about it. The structures here (the imperfect for descriptions, comparisons for places, and impersonal se for general statements about technology and the environment) reappear across all three topics, so mastering them once pays off three times over.","idea":[{"code":"Topic 1","h":"Travel and tourism, including places of interest","t":"This topic covers describing a place you have visited or would like to visit, holiday activities, and comparing destinations. The imperfect tense sets the scene for a past holiday: Cuando estaba de vacaciones, hacía mucho calor y el pueblo era muy tranquilo (When I was on holiday, the weather was very hot and the village was very peaceful). The preterite reports what you actually did: Un día visitamos un castillo antiguo y sacamos muchas fotos (One day we visited an old castle and took a lot of photos). Comparing places uses más/menos + adjective + que, or tan + adjective + como: Esta ciudad es más tranquila que mi pueblo, pero no es tan interesante como Madrid (This city is quieter than my village, but it isn't as interesting as Madrid). Be ready to describe a real place you know in the imperfect and then say one thing you did there in the preterite."},{"code":"Topic 2","h":"Media and technology","t":"This topic covers how you use technology and social media, and opinions on its advantages and disadvantages. Describe use with lo/la uso para + infinitive: Uso el móvil para sacar fotos y buscar información (I use my phone to take photos and look for information). Impersonal se is common for general statements, and the verb agrees with the noun that follows it: se puede leer el periódico (singular, el periódico) but se pueden leer las noticias (plural, las noticias). Balanced opinions use aunque or sin embargo: Aunque las redes sociales nos mantienen en contacto con amigos lejanos, también pueden reducir el contacto cara a cara (Although social media keeps us in touch with distant friends, it can also reduce face-to-face contact). Have one balanced sentence ready that weighs an advantage against a disadvantage."},{"code":"Topic 3","h":"The environment and where people live","t":"This topic covers environmental problems and solutions, and describing where you live. hay que and se debe express what needs to be done: Hay que reciclar más y usar menos plástico (We need to recycle more and use less plastic). Impersonal se also describes what people generally do: Se reciclan el vidrio y el papel cada semana en mi barrio (Glass and paper are recycled every week in my neighbourhood) — the verb agrees with the plural subject. Comparisons describe town versus country: El campo es más tranquilo que la ciudad, pero la ciudad tiene más transporte público (The countryside is quieter than the city, but the city has more public transport). Be ready to describe a town or a landscape and then say what should be done about a problem in it."}],"examples":[{"title":"Imperfect for scene-setting, preterite for events","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'The beach was beautiful and it was very hot, so on the last day we went to a fantastic restaurant.'","steps":["'The beach was beautiful' and 'it was very hot' both describe ongoing background conditions, so they take the imperfect: La playa era preciosa; hacía mucho calor.","'so' links this to what happened as a result: así que; 'on the last day' is a time phrase that will sit straight after it: el último día.","'we went to a fantastic restaurant' reports a single, one-off event, so it takes the preterite: fuimos a un restaurante fantástico.","Full sentence: La playa era preciosa y hacía mucho calor, así que el último día fuimos a un restaurante fantástico."],"cues":["Which two details describe the ongoing background of the scene, rather than an event?","Which word links the description to what happened, and how is 'on the last day' expressed?","Which tense reports a single one-off event?","How do both parts combine into the full sentence?"]},{"title":"Translating a text about using technology","setup":"Translate into English: 'Uso el móvil sobre todo para hablar con mis amigos por videollamada y para hacer los deberes en línea, aunque a veces paso demasiado tiempo en las redes sociales.'","steps":["Uso el móvil sobre todo para... translates as 'I use my phone mainly to...'.","hablar con mis amigos por videollamada y para hacer los deberes en línea gives the two uses: 'talk to my friends by video call and to do homework online'.","aunque a veces paso demasiado tiempo en las redes sociales translates as 'although sometimes I spend too much time on social media'.","Full English answer: I use my phone mainly to talk to my friends by video call and to do homework online, although sometimes I spend too much time on social media."],"cues":["What does 'sobre todo' mean here?","How would you translate 'hacer los deberes en línea'?","What does aunque introduce here?","Give the complete English translation."]},{"title":"Impersonal se with a plural subject","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'A lot of plastic bottles are recycled in this town, but more containers are needed.'","steps":["'are recycled' with a plural subject (bottles) needs se + the plural verb: se reciclan.","'a lot of plastic bottles' is muchas botellas de plástico, the subject that se reciclan agrees with.","'more containers are needed' uses the same pattern with necesitar: se necesitan más contenedores.","Full sentence: Se reciclan muchas botellas de plástico en este pueblo, pero se necesitan más contenedores."],"cues":["Is the thing being recycled singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb after se?","How is 'a lot of plastic bottles' translated, and does its number match the verb you chose?","Is the noun in the second half singular or plural, and does the verb after se match it?","Write the complete sentence, both halves joined by pero."]},{"title":"Comparing city and countryside","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I am going to move to the countryside next year, because it is quieter than the city and there is less traffic.'","steps":["'I am going to move' uses the near future: voy a mudarme.","'to the countryside next year' follows: al campo el año que viene.","'because it is quieter than the city' is a comparison after porque: porque es más tranquilo que la ciudad.","'and there is less traffic' adds a second reason with hay: y hay menos tráfico.","Full sentence: Voy a mudarme al campo el año que viene, porque es más tranquilo que la ciudad y hay menos tráfico."],"cues":["Which structure expresses a near-future plan?","How do you say 'to the countryside' and 'next year'?","Which comparison structure means 'quieter than', and what word introduces the reason?","How do you say 'there is less traffic'?","How does the whole sentence fit together?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which tense sets the scene (weather, how a place was) and which tense reports what you actually did?","a":"Imperfect for the scene; preterite for the completed actions."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'I use it to...' + an infinitive.","a":"Lo/la uso para + infinitive."},{"q":"How does the verb after se change when what follows is plural, e.g. 'bottles are recycled'?","a":"It becomes plural too: se reciclan botellas (not se recicla botellas)."},{"q":"Give the Spanish impersonal phrase for 'we need to / one must' + an infinitive.","a":"Hay que + infinitive."}]},"room":{"facts":["Imperfect sets the scene (era, hacía, había); preterite reports completed events (fuimos, visitamos, nadamos).","lo/la uso para + infinitive describes how something is used.","se + verb agrees with what follows: se puede leer el periódico (singular) but se pueden leer las noticias (plural).","hay que / se debe + infinitive express what needs to be done, without naming who must do it.","más/menos + adjective + que and tan + adjective + como compare places, exactly as they compare people."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The hotel was very comfortable.'","a":"El hotel era muy cómodo. (or: El hotel fue muy cómodo.)","sol":"El hotel era muy cómodo. The sentence describes what the hotel was like during the stay, so the imperfect era is the natural choice; fue would also be accepted, seeing the stay as a completed whole.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Is this a description of an ongoing state, or a single completed action?","Which past tense fits a description?","Subject, then the past-tense verb, then muy + an adjective agreeing with a masculine noun."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I use my laptop to do my homework.'","a":"Uso mi portátil para hacer los deberes.","sol":"Uso mi portátil para hacer los deberes. uso + para + infinitive describes the purpose of use.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which small word links 'use' to the purpose that follows?","Check the form of the verb that follows para — is it conjugated, or an infinitive?","Present-tense verb, object, then the purpose phrase with an infinitive."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'We need to protect the environment.'","a":"Hay que proteger el medio ambiente. (or: Tenemos que / Necesitamos proteger el medio ambiente.)","sol":"Hay que proteger el medio ambiente. hay que + infinitive is the impersonal 'we/you need to', with no specific subject named.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase expresses a general need, without naming who must act?","What form does the verb take right after that impersonal phrase?","Impersonal phrase, infinitive, then the object with its article."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'El año pasado, visitamos un pueblo muy pintoresco cerca de la costa.'","a":"Last year, we visited a very picturesque village near the coast.","sol":"Last year, we visited a very picturesque village near the coast. El año pasado fixes the time frame, and visitamos is the preterite of visitar, reporting a single completed action.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What time phrase opens the sentence, and what does it tell you about the tense?","What does pintoresco mean?","One English sentence: time phrase, then 'we' + past verb, then the place and its description."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Cuando era pequeño, mi familia y yo íbamos de camping todos los veranos, pero el año pasado, por primera vez, nos quedamos en un hotel de cinco estrellas.' What did the writer's family used to do, and what was different last year?","a":"They used to go camping every summer; last year, for the first time, they stayed in a five-star hotel instead.","sol":"Cuando era pequeño, mi familia y yo íbamos de camping todos los veranos — when I was young, my family and I used to go camping every summer (imperfect, a repeated past habit). Pero el año pasado, por primera vez, nos quedamos en un hotel de cinco estrellas — but last year, for the first time, we stayed in a five-star hotel (preterite, a one-off completed change).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the tense of the first verb — what does that tense say about how often the action happened?","Look for the connector that turns the sentence round, and the tense of the verb that follows it.","Two English answers: the old habit, then the one-off change."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Social media lets you stay in touch with friends, but it can also be a waste of time.'","a":"Las redes sociales te permiten mantenerte en contacto con amigos, pero también pueden ser una pérdida de tiempo.","sol":"Las redes sociales te permiten mantenerte en contacto con amigos, pero también pueden ser una pérdida de tiempo. permiten agrees with the plural subject las redes sociales; pueden ser is the modal poder followed by the infinitive ser.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Does the verb 'let/allow' agree with a singular or plural subject here?","'can also be' needs a modal verb plus an infinitive — which one?","One complete sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'A lot of rubbish is thrown in the street, which is bad for the environment.'","a":"Se tira mucha basura en la calle, lo cual (or lo que) es malo para el medio ambiente.","sol":"Se tira mucha basura en la calle, lo cual es malo para el medio ambiente. se tira agrees with the singular mucha basura; lo cual introduces a comment on the whole idea just stated, not on one specific noun.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Is 'rubbish' (basura) singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb after se?","What short phrase introduces a comment on the whole idea just stated?","One complete sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Vivo en un piso pequeño en el centro de la ciudad. Me gusta porque todo está muy cerca, pero a veces echo de menos el aire libre y el silencio del campo.' What does the writer like about their home, and what do they sometimes miss?","a":"They like that everything is very close by; they sometimes miss the open air / the outdoors and the quiet of the countryside.","sol":"Me gusta porque todo está muy cerca — I like it because everything is very close by. A veces echo de menos el aire libre y el silencio del campo — sometimes I miss the open air (the outdoors) and the quiet of the countryside.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the reason clause after porque for the first answer.","echar de menos is a fixed phrase — work out its general meaning from context.","Two English answers: the reason for liking it, then the two things missed."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'If more people used public transport, there would be less pollution in the cities.'","a":"Si más gente usara el transporte público, habría menos contaminación en las ciudades.","sol":"Si más gente usara el transporte público, habría menos contaminación en las ciudades. This is a hypothetical (unlikely/unreal) condition, so si takes the imperfect subjunctive (usara) and the result clause takes the conditional (habría), unlike a real future condition, which would use present + future.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["This is a hypothetical 'if', not a likely real one — compare it with si + present + future.","An unlikely 'if' changes the verb form in both halves — recall the pattern from the grammar sheet.","Si + clause, comma, then the result clause with its verb, object and place."],"codes":["Topic 3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Although my old phone was slower, I actually preferred it, because I spent less time distracted by notifications.'","a":"Aunque mi antiguo móvil era más lento, en realidad lo prefería, porque pasaba menos tiempo distraído por las notificaciones.","sol":"Aunque mi antiguo móvil era más lento, en realidad lo prefería, porque pasaba menos tiempo distraído por las notificaciones. era, prefería and pasaba are all imperfect, describing ongoing past states and habits rather than one-off completed actions; lo prefería uses the direct object pronoun lo for 'it' (el móvil antiguo).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Are all three verbs describing one-off completed actions, or ongoing states/habits? What tense fits that?","'I preferred it' needs a direct object pronoun for 'it' — check its gender.","Write the complete sentence with all three clauses."],"codes":["Topic 2"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about a place you have visited, covering: your general impression of it, one specific thing you did there, and whether you would like to return and why.","a":"About 90 words: an imperfect description (e.g. era un lugar precioso y hacía sol), one preterite event (e.g. visitamos el castillo), and a conditional wish with a reason (me gustaría volver porque…).","sol":"El verano pasado fui a Barcelona con mi familia. La ciudad era preciosa y había muchísima gente en las calles. Hacía mucho calor todos los días, así que llevábamos gafas de sol y una botella de agua. Un día visitamos la Sagrada Familia, que me pareció impresionante, y sacamos muchas fotos delante del edificio. Por la tarde comimos tapas en un restaurante pequeño cerca del puerto. Me gustaría volver algún día porque no tuvimos tiempo de visitar todos los museos, y también porque me encantó el ambiente de la ciudad. (90 words: era, había and hacía set the scene, fui/visitamos/comimos report events, and the final sentences give a clear opinion with reasons.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Plan the three parts in order: description, one specific event, then your opinion about returning.","Separate the scene (what the place was like) from the one thing that happened, and give each its own kind of past verb.","About 90 words in three short parts: the place, the one thing you did, then whether you would return and why."],"codes":["Topic 1"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 150 words in Spanish about technology and the environment where you live, covering: (1) how you and your family use technology day to day, with a balanced advantage and disadvantage; (2) an environmental problem in your area and one solution you think would help, explaining why.","a":"About 150 words: a balanced technology sentence (e.g. uso el móvil para…, aunque a veces…), an environmental problem in your area (e.g. hay mucha basura en las calles) with a solution using hay que or se debe, and a justified opinion.","sol":"En mi familia usamos la tecnología constantemente: mis padres trabajan desde casa con el ordenador, y yo uso el móvil para estudiar y para hablar con mis amigos. Una ventaja es que se puede encontrar información al instante, lo cual es muy útil para los deberes. Sin embargo, una desventaja es que pasamos demasiado tiempo mirando la pantalla en vez de hablar cara a cara, lo cual no es bueno para las relaciones familiares. En cuanto al medio ambiente, en mi barrio hay demasiado tráfico y el aire no es muy limpio, sobre todo por la mañana cuando todo el mundo va al trabajo en coche. Creo que la mejor solución sería mejorar el transporte público, porque si hubiera autobuses más frecuentes y más baratos, mucha más gente los usaría en vez del coche, y así habría menos contaminación en el centro de la ciudad y todos respiraríamos mejor. (149 words: everyday use, a balanced advantage/disadvantage with sin embargo, an environmental problem, and a justified solution with a hypothetical si clause.)","m":25,"d":3,"hints":["Plan two sections in the order given: technology first, then the environment.","The technology section needs a genuine balance between the advantage and the disadvantage, not just two separate opinions.","About 150 words in two parts: technology (balanced), then the environmental problem and its solution, with an opinion."],"codes":["Topic 2","Topic 3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which tense reports a single completed action, such as 'we visited'?","a":"The preterite."},{"q":"Translate: 'lo uso para sacar fotos'.","a":"I use it to take photos."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'we would need' (conditional) more public transport.","a":"Necesitaríamos más transporte público."},{"q":"What does echar de menos mean?","a":"To miss (something or someone)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"era / hacía / había (imperfecto)","back":"was / it was (weather) / there was — for describing an ongoing past scene. El pueblo era muy tranquilo. (The village was very peaceful.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"sacar fotos","back":"to take photos. Sacamos muchas fotos del castillo. (We took a lot of photos of the castle.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"pintoresco/a","back":"picturesque. Es un pueblo muy pintoresco. (It's a very picturesque village.)","code":"Topic 1","open":false},{"front":"lo/la uso para + infinitivo","back":"I use it to... Lo uso para buscar información. (I use it to look for information.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"las notificaciones","back":"notifications. Paso mucho tiempo distraído por las notificaciones. (I spend a lot of time distracted by notifications.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"mantenerse en contacto con","back":"to stay in touch with. Me permite mantenerme en contacto con amigos lejanos. (It lets me stay in touch with distant friends.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"una pérdida de tiempo","back":"a waste of time. A veces las redes sociales son una pérdida de tiempo. (Sometimes social media is a waste of time.)","code":"Topic 2","open":false},{"front":"hay que / se debe + infinitivo","back":"we/you need to. Hay que reciclar más. (We need to recycle more.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"se recicla(n)","back":"is/are recycled (agrees with what follows). Se reciclan muchas botellas. (A lot of bottles are recycled.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"el medio ambiente","back":"the environment. Hay que proteger el medio ambiente. (We need to protect the environment.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"la contaminación","back":"pollution. Hay demasiada contaminación en la ciudad. (There is too much pollution in the city.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"el campo vs la ciudad","back":"the countryside vs the city. El campo es más tranquilo que la ciudad. (The countryside is quieter than the city.)","code":"Topic 3","open":false},{"front":"Give one environmental problem and one solution, in Spanish","back":"hay mucha contaminación — hay que usar el transporte público; se tira mucha basura — se debe reciclar más; hay demasiado tráfico — se puede ir en bicicleta","code":"Topic 3","open":true},{"front":"Give one advantage and one disadvantage of social media, in Spanish","back":"te permite mantenerte en contacto con amigos — puede ser una pérdida de tiempo; puedes buscar información — a veces pasas demasiado tiempo en el móvil","code":"Topic 2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: viajes y turismo","items":["de vacaciones - on holiday","un castillo - a castle","la costa - the coast","pintoresco/a - picturesque","sacar fotos - to take photos","quedarse (en un hotel) - to stay (in a hotel)","por primera vez - for the first time","mudarse - to move (house)","cómodo/a - comfortable","el ambiente - the atmosphere"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: alojamiento y transporte","items":["el hotel - hotel","el alojamiento - accommodation","el vuelo - flight","el tren - train","la estación - station","el aeropuerto - airport","alquilar - to rent, hire","una excursión - a trip, excursion"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: tecnología","items":["el portátil - the laptop","las redes sociales - social media","mantenerse en contacto - to stay in touch","una pérdida de tiempo - a waste of time","las notificaciones - notifications","la pantalla - the screen","en línea - online"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: ventajas y desventajas de la tecnología","items":["una ventaja - an advantage","una desventaja - a disadvantage","la videollamada - video call","buscar información - to look for information","distraído/a - distracted","adicto/a (a) - addicted (to)","cara a cara - face to face","demasiado tiempo - too much time"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: el medio ambiente","items":["reciclar / el reciclaje - to recycle / recycling","el medio ambiente - the environment","la contaminación - pollution","el transporte público - public transport","un contenedor - a container, bin","el campo / la ciudad - the countryside / the city","la basura - rubbish"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: dónde vivo","items":["el piso - flat","el barrio - neighbourhood","el aire libre - the open air","el silencio - silence","el tráfico - traffic","tranquilo/a - quiet, peaceful","echar de menos - to miss (something/someone)","mudarse - to move (house)"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: imperfecto, se impersonal y el condicional","items":["el imperfecto para descripciones y hábitos. Example: El pueblo era muy tranquilo y hacía sol todos los días. (The village was very peaceful and it was sunny every day.)","el pretérito para acciones puntuales y terminadas. Example: Un día visitamos un castillo y sacamos muchas fotos. (One day we visited a castle and took lots of photos.)","se + verbo (concuerda con lo que sigue). Example: Se puede leer el periódico, pero se pueden leer también las noticias en línea. (You can read the newspaper, but you can also read the news online.)","hay que / se debe + infinitivo, sin nombrar a quién. Example: Hay que reciclar más y usar menos plástico. (We need to recycle more and use less plastic.)","si + imperfecto de subjuntivo, + condicional (hipótesis poco probable). Example: Si usara el transporte público, contaminaría menos. (If I used public transport, I would pollute less.)","el condicional regular: hablaría, comería, viviría; irregular solo en la raíz de unos pocos verbos (habría, de haber; sería es la forma regular de ser). Example: Con más autobuses, habría menos contaminación. (With more buses, there would be less pollution.)","comparaciones con lugares: más/menos + adjetivo + que; tan + adjetivo + como. Example: El campo es tan bonito como la costa. (The countryside is as pretty as the coast.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.539Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.84,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly (including the 90- and 149-word model paragraphs, which match their stated lengths and the 15- and 25-mark writing shapes), so the main fixes are small: reword the 'basura' hint, tighten two leaky cues, and either correct or replace the compound-subject 'se reciclan' example in the Topic 3 lesson section."}}},"3.2.1.1":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.1.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Noun phrases are the building blocks of almost every Spanish sentence you write or speak, so small mistakes here (a wrong gender ending, a missed plural, an article left out where Spanish requires one) recur across every paper. This room drills six specific patterns the specification names: how feminine and plural nouns are formed, using the infinitive as a noun, articles and their contraction, other determiners that must agree, and pronoun forms and word order.","idea":[{"code":"Formation of feminine nouns","h":"Making a noun feminine","t":"Most nouns ending in -o change to -a for the feminine: vecino → vecina (neighbour), camarero → camarera (waiter/waitress). Nouns ending in -or add -a: profesor → profesora, director → directora. Nouns ending in -ante, -ente or -ista do not change for gender: el/la estudiante, el/la artista — only the article shows the gender. Some nouns are simply different words for each gender, not formed by a rule, and must be learned individually: hijo (son) / hija (daughter). Job titles are shifting in usage, and both el jefe/la jefe and la jefa are accepted for 'the (female) boss'."},{"code":"Formation of plural nouns","h":"Making a noun plural","t":"A noun ending in a vowel adds -s: casa → casas, libro → libros. A noun ending in a consonant adds -es: profesor → profesores, ciudad → ciudades. A noun ending in -z changes z to c before adding -es: lápiz → lápices, vez → veces. A noun ending in -(i)ón adds -es and drops its written accent, because the stress now falls where the rule predicts without one: canción → canciones. The rule never moves the stress of the word: profesor → profesores keeps its stress on -so-. Genuinely irregular plurals are not formed by rule at all — they are given in the vocabulary list and learned as entries."},{"code":"Infinitive used as a noun","h":"The infinitive as a noun, and nationality words as nouns","t":"Spanish uses the plain infinitive where English uses the -ing form as a noun: Fumar es malo para la salud (Smoking is bad for your health); Me encanta viajar (I love travelling). A masculine nationality or language adjective can also work as a noun: el francés, with its article, can mean either 'French' (the language) or 'the Frenchman', depending on context; nationality adjectives inflect regularly for the feminine and plural in the usual ways: inglés → inglesa (feminine), españoles (plural masculine or mixed group)."},{"code":"Articles","h":"Definite and indefinite articles, and their contraction","t":"The definite article (el, la, los, las) and indefinite article (un, una, unos, unas) both agree with the noun for gender and number. Spanish uses the definite article in some places English omits it, especially for general statements: La educación es importante (Education is important); Me gusta el chocolate (I like chocolate). El contracts with the prepositions de and a: de + el → del (la casa del profesor, the teacher's house); a + el → al (Vamos al cine, we're going to the cinema) — la, los and las never contract."},{"code":"Other determiners","h":"Demonstratives, indefinites and possessives that agree","t":"Demonstrative adjectives agree for gender and number: este/esta/estos/estas (this/these, near me), ese/esa/esos/esas (that/those, near you). Indefinite adjectives that agree include mismo/misma (same), otro/otra (other, another — never with un before it: otro libro, not un otro libro), and todo/toda (all, every); cada (each, every) never changes at all. algún and ningún (before a masculine singular noun: algún día, ningún problema) are the forms actually tested, along with their agreeing forms alguna, algunos, algunas, ninguna — the base forms alguno and ninguno themselves are listed only as the entry these forms build from, not separately tested here. Possessive adjectives agree with the thing owned, not the owner: mi/mis (my), tu/tus (your, informal singular), su/sus (his/her/its/your-formal/their) — none of these three change for gender — nuestro/nuestra/nuestros/nuestras (our) and vuestro/vuestra/vuestros/vuestras (your, informal plural), the two sets that do have a separate feminine form."},{"code":"Pronouns","h":"Subject pronouns, object pronoun word order, and other pronoun forms","t":"Subject pronouns (yo, tú, él, ella, nosotros/nosotras, vosotros/vosotras, ellos/ellas, usted, ustedes) and the invariable pronouns algo (something) and alguien (someone) are listed; subject pronouns are normally omitted, since the verb ending already shows who is meant; they are used mainly to change subject or add emphasis: Yo prefiero el té, pero él prefiere el café (I prefer tea, but he prefers coffee). Object and reflexive pronouns (me, te, lo/la, los/las, le/les, se) go before a single conjugated verb, or, with two verbs, either before the first or attached to the infinitive — but never both at once: Lo miro; Lo puedo mirar / Puedo mirarlo; with a positive command, the pronoun always attaches to the end: ¡Míralo! Interrogative pronouns include cuál/cuáles, cuánto (which inflects for gender and number: cuánto, cuánta, cuántos, cuántas) and quién/quiénes. que introduces a subject relative clause: la chica que vive aquí (the girl who lives here). esto and eso are neuter demonstrative pronouns for a whole idea rather than one noun: Eso no me gusta (I don't like that). alguno and ninguno can also stand alone as pronouns, inflecting for gender and number: Tengo tres primos; alguno de ellos vive en Madrid (I have three cousins; one of them lives in Madrid)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Forming feminine and plural nouns together","setup":"Give the feminine plural forms of: profesor (teacher), and the plural of lápiz (pencil).","steps":["profesor ends in -or, so the feminine is formed by adding -a: profesora.","For the plural of a feminine noun ending in -a (a vowel), simply add -s: profesoras.","lápiz ends in -z, so z changes to c before adding -es.","Full answer: profesoras; lápices."],"cues":["What ending does profesor have, and what does that ending add for the feminine?","Once you have profesora, which plural rule applies — vowel or consonant ending?","What special change happens to a word ending in -z before adding -es?","What are the two final forms, and which rule produced each?"]},{"title":"The infinitive as a noun, with an opinion","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'Reading is more interesting than watching television.'","steps":["'Reading' as the subject of the sentence is the plain infinitive: Leer.","'is more interesting than' is a comparison: es más interesante que.","'watching television' is also an infinitive used as a noun: ver la televisión.","Full sentence: Leer es más interesante que ver la televisión."],"cues":["What form of the verb translates '-ing' as the subject of a sentence in Spanish?","Which comparison structure means 'more … than'?","What form does 'watching' take here, matching the first word of the sentence?","How do the three parts fit together in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Article contraction with de and a","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'We're going to the park, and then to the house of the neighbour.'","steps":["'to the park' uses a + el, which contracts to al: al parque.","'We're going' is vamos.","'to the house of the neighbour' uses de + el for possession, contracting to del: la casa del vecino.","Full sentence: Vamos al parque, y luego a la casa del vecino."],"cues":["Which two words contract when a is followed by el?","What is 'we're going' in Spanish?","Which two words contract when de is followed by el?","What is the complete sentence once both contractions are in place?"]},{"title":"Object pronoun position with a two-verb construction","setup":"Give both correct word orders for: 'I want to buy it' (referring to a book, el libro).","steps":["The object is lo (masculine, standing for el libro).","With a conjugated verb (quiero) plus an infinitive (comprar), lo can attach to the infinitive: quiero comprarlo.","Or lo can go before the conjugated verb instead: lo quiero comprar.","Both correct answers: Quiero comprarlo. / Lo quiero comprar."],"cues":["Which pronoun stands for el libro (masculine)?","Attach the pronoun to the infinitive first — what do you get?","Now move the same pronoun in front of the conjugated verb instead — what changes?","Which two positions did the pronoun take, and are both accepted?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How do nouns ending in -ante, -ente or -ista show gender?","a":"They don't change form — only the article (el/la) shows the gender."},{"q":"What happens to the written accent when a word ending in -ión becomes plural?","a":"It is dropped: canción → canciones."},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'Smoking is bad for your health', using the infinitive as a noun.","a":"Fumar es malo para la salud."},{"q":"Where does an object pronoun go with a positive command, such as 'look at it'?","a":"Attached to the end of the command: Míralo."}]},"room":{"facts":["Feminine: -o → -a (vecino/vecina); -or adds -a (profesor/profesora); -ante/-ente/-ista do not change.","Plural: vowel adds -s; consonant adds -es; -z changes to -c before -es; -ión drops its accent in the plural.","de + el = del; a + el = al. la, los and las never contract.","Object/reflexive pronouns go before a single conjugated verb, or before/attached-to an infinitive in a two-verb construction, but always attach to a positive command.","Possessive adjectives agree with the thing owned; nuestro/nuestra and vuestro/vuestra are the two sets with their own feminine form (mi, tu and su do not change for gender)."],"questions":[{"q":"Give the feminine form of vecino (neighbour).","a":"vecina","sol":"vecina — a noun ending in -o simply changes to -a for the feminine.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Which ending does vecino have?","Which rule applies to that ending?","One word."],"codes":["Formation of feminine nouns"]},{"q":"Give the plural of canción (song).","a":"canciones","sol":"canciones — a word ending in -ión adds -es and drops its written accent, since the stress now falls where the rule predicts without one.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Does this word end in a vowel or a consonant?","What happens to the accent on a word like this in the plural?","One word."],"codes":["Formation of plural nouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Swimming is good exercise.'","a":"Nadar es buen ejercicio.","sol":"Nadar es buen ejercicio. The infinitive nadar is used as the subject noun, matching the English '-ing' form used as a subject. Nadar es un buen ejercicio (with the article) is equally correct.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What form of the verb 'to swim' acts as the subject here?","This is the same pattern as 'reading', 'smoking' and other '-ing' subjects.","One complete sentence."],"codes":["Infinitive used as a noun"]},{"q":"Complete: 'Vamos ___ parque este fin de semana.'","a":"al","sol":"Vamos al parque este fin de semana. a + el always contracts to al.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Two small words are combining into one here.","Which two small words fuse when they meet here?","One word."],"codes":["Articles"]},{"q":"Give the plural of 'ese problema' (that problem), bearing in mind that problema is masculine despite ending in -a.","a":"esos problemas","sol":"esos problemas. problema is masculine despite ending in -a, so its demonstrative must be masculine: ese problema → esos problemas.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Check the real gender of problema — endings in -a are not always feminine.","Which plural form of ese matches a masculine noun?","Give the two-word plural phrase."],"codes":["Other determiners"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My sister has two keys, but I don't want either of them.'","a":"Mi hermana tiene dos llaves, pero no quiero ninguna. (or: … no quiero ninguna de ellas.)","sol":"Mi hermana tiene dos llaves, pero no quiero ninguna. ninguna stands alone as a pronoun for llaves (feminine), agreeing in gender even though no noun follows; no goes before the verb.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which pronoun means 'not any/neither of them', and what does it agree with?","Where does no go in the second clause?","Two clauses joined by pero."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This house belongs to the teacher, and that car belongs to the neighbour.'","a":"Esta casa es del profesor (or: de la profesora), y ese coche es del vecino.","sol":"Esta casa es del profesor, y ese coche es del vecino. esta and ese are demonstrative adjectives agreeing with casa (feminine) and coche (masculine); 'belongs to the teacher/neighbour' is ser + de + el, contracted to del.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["'belongs to' is expressed with a verb plus a preposition — what happens when that preposition meets el?","Match each demonstrative to the gender of its noun.","Two clauses joined by y."],"codes":["Articles","Other determiners"]},{"q":"Rewrite, moving the object pronoun to the other correct position: 'Puedo verte mañana.'","a":"Te puedo ver mañana.","sol":"Te puedo ver mañana. With puedo (conjugated) plus ver (infinitive), the pronoun te may attach to the infinitive or move before the conjugated verb — both correct; the task asks for the other option.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Which two verbs are in this sentence, and which is conjugated?","The pronoun can move to the other side of the two verbs.","Move only the pronoun; keep mañana at the end."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the infinitive as a noun: 'Learning languages opens many doors.'","a":"Aprender idiomas abre muchas puertas.","sol":"Aprender idiomas abre muchas puertas. Aprender is the infinitive used as the subject; abre is the él/ella form of abrir, agreeing with the singular subject aprender idiomas (one activity), not with the plural idiomas.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["What form of 'to learn' is the subject of this sentence?","Does the verb agree with idiomas (plural) or with the activity as a whole (singular)?","One complete sentence."],"codes":["Infinitive used as a noun"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The female teachers are nice, but their songs are difficult.'","a":"Las profesoras son simpáticas, pero sus canciones son difíciles.","sol":"Las profesoras son simpáticas, pero sus canciones son difíciles. profesor takes -a for the feminine and then -s for the plural (profesoras); canción ends in a consonant, so it adds -es and drops its written accent (canciones); sus agrees with the plural noun owned, not with the owners; the adjectives agree with each noun.","hints":["Two nouns need their form changed — one for gender and number, one for number only.","For each noun, apply the ending rule, then check whether a written accent survives.","Article + noun + verb + adjective, pero, possessive + noun + verb + adjective."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["Formation of feminine nouns","Formation of plural nouns","Other determiners"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Each student has a book, and someone wants to share it.'","a":"Cada estudiante tiene un libro, y alguien quiere compartirlo. (or: … alguien lo quiere compartir.)","sol":"Cada estudiante tiene un libro, y alguien quiere compartirlo. cada never changes form; alguien is the invariable pronoun 'someone'; compartirlo attaches the object pronoun lo to the infinitive.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["'Each' before a noun uses a determiner that never changes its ending.","Which invariable pronoun means 'someone'?","Where can the object pronoun attach in the final clause?"],"codes":["Other determiners","Pronouns"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Mi profesora tiene dos gatos, y su casa está cerca del parque.' Answer in English: what pets does the teacher have, and where is her house?","a":"Two cats; near the park.","sol":"Mi profesora tiene dos gatos, y su casa está cerca del parque — My teacher has two cats, and her house is near the park. profesora shows the -or/-ora feminine rule; su agrees with casa (the thing owned), not with the teacher.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Find what the teacher has, straight after the verb tiene.","Find the phrase describing where the house is.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Formation of feminine nouns","Other determiners"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Viajar es caro, pero la educación es más importante que el dinero.'","a":"Travelling is expensive, but education is more important than money.","sol":"Viajar es caro, pero la educación es más importante que el dinero — Travelling is expensive, but education is more important than money. Viajar is the infinitive used as a subject noun; la educación uses the definite article for a general statement, as Spanish requires.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["What form of 'to travel' is the subject of the first clause?","Why does educación need an article here even though English says just 'education'?","Give one complete English sentence."],"codes":["Infinitive used as a noun","Articles"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about your family and your home, using at least one feminine noun formed with a rule from this room, one plural noun formed with a rule from this room, an infinitive used as a noun, and one possessive adjective, referring to the present, the past and the future.","a":"A paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish, correctly using at least one feminine and one plural noun formed by the rules in this room, an infinitive as a noun, a possessive adjective, and verbs across present, past and future time frames.","sol":"Vivo con mis padres y mi hermana menor en una casa pequeña cerca del centro. Mi madre es profesora y mi padre es camarero; los dos trabajan muchas horas, así que la cocina es una habitación muy importante en nuestra casa. El año pasado nos mudamos a este barrio, y al principio no conocíamos a nadie, pero ahora tenemos buenos vecinos. Cocinar juntos los domingos es nuestra tradición favorita. El año que viene voy a mudarme a una casa más grande, con un jardín para nuestro perro. (87 words: profesora gives the feminine-noun rule, vecinos gives the plural-noun rule, cocinar is the infinitive as noun, mis/mi/nuestra are possessive adjectives, and vivo/nos mudamos/voy a mudarme cover present, past and future.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Which noun rules from this room can you show in one sentence about your family?","One activity your family does can be expressed with an infinitive as the subject.","A past verb for when you moved, a present one for now, and ir a + infinitive for next year."],"codes":["Formation of feminine nouns","Formation of plural nouns","Infinitive used as a noun","Other determiners"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Las canciones de mi profesora son muy alegres, pero no me gusta ninguna de sus clases.' Answer in English: what are the teacher's songs like, and what does the writer think of her classes?","a":"They are very cheerful; the writer doesn't like any of her classes.","sol":"Las canciones de mi profesora son muy alegres — the teacher's songs are very cheerful (canciones is the plural of canción, accent dropped; profesora is the feminine of profesor); no me gusta ninguna de sus clases — the writer doesn't like any of her classes (ninguna agrees with clases).","hints":["Find the adjective after son in the first half.","The second half is negative — find the word that says how many classes are liked.","Two short English answers, one per half."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Formation of plural nouns","Formation of feminine nouns","Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Viajar en tren es más barato que ir al aeropuerto.'","a":"Travelling by train is cheaper than going to the airport.","sol":"Viajar en tren — travelling by train (an infinitive used as the subject, translated with the English -ing form); es más barato que — is cheaper than; ir al aeropuerto — going to the airport (al = a + el; the second infinitive is also translated with -ing).","hints":["Two infinitives are doing the job of nouns here — how does English express that?","Which two small words have fused into al?","One complete English sentence with a comparison."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Infinitive used as a noun","Articles"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the feminine of director.","a":"directora"},{"q":"Give the plural of vez (time, occasion).","a":"veces"},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'the same colour', using mismo.","a":"el mismo color"},{"q":"Where does an object pronoun attach in a positive command like '¡Mírala!'?","a":"To the end of the command."}]},"cards":[{"front":"vecina","back":"neighbour (f.) — vecino → vecina.","code":"Formation of feminine nouns","open":false},{"front":"profesora","back":"teacher (f.) — profesor adds -a.","code":"Formation of feminine nouns","open":false},{"front":"la estudiante","back":"the student (f.) — -ante nouns don't change, only the article shows gender.","code":"Formation of feminine nouns","open":false},{"front":"las canciones","back":"the songs — -ión adds -es and drops its accent.","code":"Formation of plural nouns","open":false},{"front":"los lápices","back":"the pencils — -z changes to -c before -es.","code":"Formation of plural nouns","open":false},{"front":"fumar","back":"to smoke, smoking — Fumar es malo. (Smoking is bad.)","code":"Infinitive used as a noun","open":false},{"front":"la educación","back":"education — Spanish keeps the article for general statements: La educación es importante.","code":"Articles","open":false},{"front":"del / al","back":"of the / to the — de+el and a+el always contract.","code":"Articles","open":false},{"front":"cada estudiante","back":"each student — cada never changes form.","code":"Other determiners","open":false},{"front":"otro libro","back":"another book — never used with un/una before it.","code":"Other determiners","open":false},{"front":"su libro","back":"his/her/their book — agrees with the thing owned, not the owner.","code":"Other determiners","open":false},{"front":"¿Quién vive aquí?","back":"Who lives here? — interrogative pronoun quién.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"la chica que vive aquí","back":"the girl who lives here — que introduces a subject relative clause.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"eso","back":"that — neuter pronoun for a whole idea, not one noun.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"Yo prefiero el té","back":"I prefer tea — the subject pronoun yo is normally omitted, used here for contrast/emphasis.","code":"Pronouns","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: sustantivos con formación regular e irregular","items":["el hijo / la hija - son / daughter","el jefe / la jefe(a) - boss","el profesor / la profesora - teacher","el camarero / la camarera - waiter / waitress","el estudiante / la estudiante - student","el artista / la artista - artist","el francés / la francesa - Frenchman / Frenchwoman","el lápiz (los lápices) - pencil(s)","la canción (las canciones) - song(s)","el examen (los exámenes) - exam(s)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: determinantes y pronombres","items":["este/esta/estos/estas - this/these","ese/esa/esos/esas - that/those","mismo/misma - same","otro/otra - other, another","cada - each, every","algún/alguna/algunos/algunas - some, any","ningún/ninguna - none, not any","cuál/cuánto/quién - which/how much/who","alguien / algo - someone / something","alguno/a (de ellos) - one (of them)"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: frases nominales de Foundation tier","items":["Femenino: -o → -a; -or → -ora; -ante/-ente/-ista sin cambio. Example: Mi vecina es profesora. (My neighbour is a teacher.)","Plural: vocal + -s; consonante + -es; -z → -ces; -ión pierde el acento escrito. Example: Los exámenes y las canciones son difíciles. (The exams and the songs are difficult.)","El infinitivo como sustantivo. Example: Viajar es divertido, pero cuesta dinero. (Travelling is fun, but it costs money.)","Contracción: de + el = del; a + el = al. Example: Vamos al parque y luego a la casa del vecino. (We're going to the park and then to the neighbour's house.)","Posesivos: mi/mis, tu/tus, su/sus (sin cambio de género), nuestro/a/os/as, vuestro/a/os/as (con forma femenina). Example: Nuestra casa es más grande que su casa. (Our house is bigger than his house.)","Pronombres de objeto: antes del verbo conjugado, o antes/pegado al infinitivo; siempre pegado al imperativo afirmativo. Example: Lo quiero comprar. / Quiero comprarlo. / ¡Cómpralo! (I want to buy it. / Buy it!)","que en cláusulas relativas de sujeto. Example: La chica que vive aquí es mi amiga. (The girl who lives here is my friend.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.540Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solved correctly and the Spanish throughout (canciones, lápices, esos problemas, compartirlo/lo quiere compartir, abre with the infinitive subject) is sound; fix the 'education' hint, the give-away stem in the ese problema question and the 'ir a + infinitive' hint before publishing."}}},"3.2.1.2":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.1.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"This room covers the grammar of the verb phrase and the words that build around it at Foundation tier: how to negate a verb, ask a question, choose the right tense/person form, order words with interesar-type verbs, use modals and reflexives, build adjective and adverb phrases with their comparatives, use prepositions correctly, and recognise (in reading only) words built from a listed base by a small set of regular patterns. These are the structures every Foundation sentence is built from, so getting the word order and person restrictions right affects almost every question on both papers.","idea":[{"code":"Negation","h":"Negation: no plus nunca, nadie, ninguno","t":"Verbal negation places no directly before the verb. A second negative word — nada ('nothing'), nunca ('never'), nadie ('nobody/anybody'), or ninguno ('none/not any') — is then placed after the verb: No veo a nadie, No como nunca carne. Alternatively, nada, nunca, nadie or ninguno can be placed before the verb on their own, without no: Nunca como carne. Both orders are correct and mean the same thing; what is not correct is dropping no when the second negative word comes after the verb."},{"code":"Interrogatives","h":"Interrogatives: intonation and wh-word plus verb-subject order","t":"Questions can be formed purely through rising intonation on a statement, shown in writing by opening and closing question marks: ¿Vives aquí? When a wh-word is used (qué, quién, cuándo, por qué, cómo, cuál, cuánto, dónde), it is followed by the verb and then the subject, if the subject is stated at all: ¿Dónde está Daniel? and ¿Qué hace la chica? Very often, especially with a verb already understood from context, the overt subject is left out altogether: ¿Dónde vives?"},{"code":"Inflectional morphology","h":"Verb tenses and persons: full six-person tenses versus singular-only tenses","t":"Present indicative, present continuous, preterite, present perfect and the periphrastic future (ir a + infinitive) are all required across every person, 1st to 3rd, singular and plural, including the irregular forms of estar, hacer, ir, ser, tener and the irregular preterite stems (tuve, pude, hice, vine, estuve, puse, quise, dije, traje) listed in the vocabulary list. By contrast, the imperfect, imperfect continuous, inflectional future, conditional and the imperative are only required for 1st to 3rd person singular (and, for the imperative, only the 2nd person singular tú form) — students are not expected to produce the plural forms of these five. Spelling changes made purely to preserve pronunciation (present g→j, preterite c→qu, g→gu, z→c, or the added y in 3rd persons) are not credit-bearing on their own. Stem changes fall into five clusters you must recognise and produce: o→ue (encontrar → encuentro, poder → puedo), e→ie (pensar → pienso, querer → quiero), e→i (pedir → pido), c→zc in the yo form (conocer → conozco) and an added g in the yo form (poner → pongo; tener → tengo is listed separately as an irregular verb). tener + noun covers states where English uses 'to be' + adjective: tengo frío (I am cold), tiene hambre (she is hungry), tengo quince años (I am fifteen). The present perfect is formed with the present of haber (he, has, ha, hemos, habéis, han) plus the past participle, -ado for -ar verbs and -ido for -er/-ir verbs: he hablado, hemos comido, han vivido. The singular-only tenses have their own irregular forms to learn: in the imperfect, era, iba and veía alongside the regular tenía, había and estaba; in the future and conditional, the four irregular stems tendr-, har-, podr- and pondr- (tendré, haría, podría, pondrá) plus habrá / habría; and the affirmative tú imperative of the listed irregular verbs."},{"code":"Syntax of interesar-type verbs","h":"Interesar-type verbs: Object Verb Subject word order","t":"Verbs like interesar pattern like gustar: the person interested is expressed as an indirect object pronoun (me, te, le, nos, os, les) placed before the verb, and the thing that interests them is the grammatical subject, which comes after the verb and controls its person/number: Me interesa la música (singular subject, singular verb) but Me interesan los deportes (plural subject, plural verb). This Object-Verb-Subject order is the reverse of normal English word order ('I am interested in sport'), which is why it needs separate attention."},{"code":"Modals, for 1st, 2nd and 3rd persons in singular and plural","h":"Modals plus infinitive, and two fixed singular phrases","t":"The modal verbs deber, poder, querer, tener que and saber are each followed directly by an infinitive, and are required across all six persons (1st to 3rd, singular and plural): Debemos estudiar, Pueden venir, Tiene que trabajar. Two multi-word fixed phrases sit inside the same entry: quisiera + infinitive ('I/he/she/you(usted) would like to') and me/te/le gustaría + infinitive ('I/you/he/she would like to'). Quisiera does not change its form, and the pronoun before gustaría shows who would like to: Quisiera visitar Madrid; Le gustaría estudiar en España."},{"code":"Reflexive use of verbs","h":"Reflexive verbs: singular persons only, and when the reflexive form is separately listed","t":"Reflexive verb use at Foundation tier is required for 1st, 2nd and 3rd person singular only. Where a reflexive form has the same meaning as its listed base verb (for example lavar 'to wash' and lavarse 'to wash oneself'), the reflexive form is not given its own entry in the vocabulary list — it is assumed from the base verb. Where the reflexive form has a different meaning from the base verb (for example poner 'to put' and ponerse 'to become/put on'), it is listed separately on the same line as the base verb, and if it is not listed in this way, it will not be tested."},{"code":"Impersonal verbs","h":"Impersonal verbs: hay, hay que, se puede/se necesita, and hace for weather","t":"Hay ('there is/are') is used without changing for person or number. Hay que + infinitive expresses a general obligation ('it is necessary to'). The impersonal se, combined with puede or necesita, expresses a generalised 'you/one' meaning: se puede reciclar ('you can recycle'), se necesita ahorrar ('one needs to save'). Weather expressions use hace + a noun from the vocabulary list, such as hace sol ('it's sunny') or hace frío ('it's cold') — note this is hace with a noun, not an adjective as English 'it's sunny' might suggest."},{"code":"Adjectival phrases","h":"Adjective agreement, position, ser/estar and the comparative menos...que","t":"Adjectives agree with the noun they describe: most follow an -o/-a/-os/-as pattern for gender and number, some only change for number (-e/-es, -z/-ces, -ista/-istas), and nationality adjectives ending in a consonant add -esa/-es/-esas or -a/-es/-as. Most adjectives follow the noun, but a defined set (algún, ningún, primer, segundo, tercer, buen, mal, gran) and a set that changes meaning depending on position (such as único) can come before it. Ser and estar combine with adjectives differently, sometimes changing meaning (listo/a = 'clever' with ser, 'ready' with estar). The comparative structure listed for adjectives is menos...que ('less...than'), with mejor and peor as the irregular comparatives."},{"code":"Adverbial phrases","h":"Adverb position and the comparatives más/menos...que, tan...como","t":"Adverbs and adverbial phrases of time, manner and place are listed in the vocabulary list, and you need to control where they go. Adverbs of time (hoy, siempre, ahora) usually open the sentence or come straight before the verb: Hoy no tengo clase; Siempre desayuno en casa. Adverbs of manner (bien, mal, rápidamente, despacio) follow the verb and its object: Habla español muy bien; Hago los deberes rápidamente. Adverbs of place (aquí, allí, cerca) come after the verb or at the end of the sentence: Vivo aquí; Mis abuelos viven cerca. Adverbs compare with más...que ('more...than'), menos...que ('less...than') or tan...como ('as...as'): Mi hermana habla más despacio que yo; Corro tan rápidamente como tú. The irregular comparatives mejor and peor serve adverbs and adjectives alike: Canta mejor que yo."},{"code":"Prepositions","h":"Prepositions: personal a, de for possession, para/sin plus infinitive, and verb+preposition combinations","t":"Prepositions are listed in the vocabulary list. The personal a is required before a direct object that is a specific person: Veo a Ana. De expresses possession: la casa de Hugo ('Hugo's house'). Para and sin can be followed directly by an infinitive: para aprender ('in order to learn'), sin hablar ('without speaking'). Some verbs need a specific preposition before an infinitive or noun, changing or adding to their meaning, and these are listed alongside the verb entry: dejar de + infinitive ('to stop doing'), ir de + noun ('to go on/for'), llegar a + infinitive ('to manage to')."},{"code":"Derivational morphology (Reading only)","h":"Derived forms allowed in reading texts even when only the base form is listed","t":"If only a base word is listed in the vocabulary list (for example libro, 'book'), a form derived from it using one of the regular patterns covered in this room (for example librito, 'little book') may still appear in a reading text, and students are expected to work out its meaning. The same applies in reverse: if only the derived form is listed, the base form may appear in reading. This reading-only allowance does not extend to listening material or to what students must produce themselves — if a derived form is required for listening or production, it is listed in the vocabulary list in its own right."},{"code":"Uniformly applicable derivational morphology that changes meaning","h":"-ito/-ita ('little'/affection) and -ísimo/-ísima ('very')","t":"Adding -ito or -ita to a noun (removing a final -o or -a first if the noun has one) always narrows its meaning to 'little' (librito, from libro) or adds a sense of affection or endearment (hermanito, from hermano, 'little brother' in a fond sense) — this pattern applies uniformly wherever it is used. Adding -ísimo or -ísima to an adjective (again removing a final -o or -a first if present) always means 'very' or 'extremely': facilísimo (from fácil, which ends in a consonant, so nothing is removed) means 'extremely easy'."},{"code":"Other high frequency patterns","h":"-mente ('-ly'), -idad ('-ity') and -able ('-able')","t":"Three further high-frequency suffixes change a word's part of speech rather than just its degree of meaning. Adding -mente to the feminine singular form of an adjective (or directly to an adjective that has only one form, like alegre) makes an adverb meaning '-ly': normalmente ('normally'), from normal. Adding -idad to an adjective, removing a final vowel if it has one, makes a noun meaning '-(i)ty': seguridad ('security'), from seguro. Adding -able to a verb stem makes an adjective meaning '-able': evitable ('avoidable'), from evitar."}],"examples":[{"title":"Negating a sentence with a personal object","setup":"Translate into Spanish, using the correct negative word order: 'I never speak to anybody at school.'","steps":["Nunca can go directly before the verb instead of using no...nunca after it: Nunca hablo.","'Speak to somebody' in Spanish is hablar con alguien, so 'to nobody' after a negative is con nadie.","Add the location: en el colegio.","Answer: Nunca hablo con nadie en el colegio."],"cues":["Which negative word can start the sentence so that no is not needed?","Which preposition does hablar take before the person spoken to?","Where does the location go?","What is the complete sentence?"]},{"title":"Building a wh-question with subject-verb inversion","setup":"Translate into Spanish, forming a question with the correct word order after the wh-word: 'Where does Ana live?'","steps":["The wh-word for 'where' is dónde, and Spanish questions open and close with question marks: ¿Dónde...?","The verb is vivir ('to live'); in the 3rd person singular this is vive.","After the wh-word, the verb comes first and the subject Ana follows it: ¿Dónde vive Ana?"],"cues":["What is the Spanish word for 'where', and how do written questions begin and end?","What is 'to live' in Spanish, and what form does it take for Ana?","What is left to place, and where does it go?"]},{"title":"Comparing two adjectives","setup":"Translate into Spanish, using the comparative structure for adjectives: 'This film is less interesting than that one.'","steps":["The adjective is interesante ('interesting'); it already ends in -e, so it stays the same for gender and only changes for number, which is not needed here.","The comparative pattern for 'less...than' with adjectives is menos + adjective + que: menos interesante que.","'This film' is esta película, and 'that one' (referring back to a feminine noun, película) is esa.","Answer: Esta película es menos interesante que esa."],"cues":["Does the adjective interesante change its ending here?","What is the Spanish comparative structure for 'less...than' with an adjective?","How do you say 'this film' and 'that one' in Spanish?","Give the complete comparative sentence."]},{"title":"Comparing two adverbs","setup":"Translate into Spanish, using the comparative structure for adverbs: 'My brother runs faster than me.'","steps":["The adverb is rápidamente ('fast/quickly'), formed by adding -mente to rápida, the feminine singular form of rápido.","The comparative pattern for 'more...than' with adverbs is más + adverb + que: más rápidamente que.","'My brother runs' is Mi hermano corre, and after que, the subject pronoun yo (not mí) is used for 'than me'.","Answer: Mi hermano corre más rápidamente que yo."],"cues":["How do you form an adverb meaning 'quickly' from an adjective ending in -o?","What is the Spanish comparative structure for 'more...than' with an adverb?","What pronoun follows que when comparing with 'me'?","Give the complete comparative sentence."]},{"title":"'Would like to' with the correct pronoun","setup":"Translate into Spanish, using the fixed phrase gustaría plus infinitive: 'She would like to travel to Spain.'","steps":["The fixed phrase for 'would like to' is le gustaría + infinitive, using le for the 3rd person singular indirect object pronoun 'she'.","The infinitive needed is viajar ('to travel').","'To Spain' is a España.","Answer: Le gustaría viajar a España."],"cues":["Which indirect object pronoun goes with 'she' in the gustaría construction?","What is the infinitive for 'to travel'?","How do you say 'to Spain'?","Give the complete sentence."]},{"title":"Three tenses in one short paragraph","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'Yesterday I did my homework, but today I have to go out because my sister has arrived.'","steps":["'I did' is a completed past action, so it takes the preterite, and hacer has an irregular stem in the yo form: hice.","'I have to go out' is the modal tener que + infinitive, and tener adds g in the yo form: tengo que salir.","'my sister has arrived' is the present perfect: the present of haber plus the participle in -ado — ha llegado.","Full sentence: Ayer hice los deberes, pero hoy tengo que salir porque mi hermana ha llegado."],"cues":["Which past tense fits a finished action like 'did', and is hacer regular in it?","Which modal expresses 'have to', and what happens to tener in the yo form?","How is 'has arrived' built from haber and a participle?","How do the three parts join into one sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What word order do no...nunca and no...nadie share when negating a verb, and what is the alternative order?","a":"No comes before the verb, with nunca or nadie after it; alternatively, nunca or nadie can go before the verb on their own, without no."},{"q":"In a Spanish question built with a wh-word, what word order typically follows the wh-word?","a":"Verb, then subject (if the subject is stated at all — it is often left out)."},{"q":"Which comparative word goes with adjectives for 'less...than', and which additional words go with adverbs?","a":"menos...que for adjectives; adverbs also use más...que and tan...como, with mejor and peor irregular in both sets."},{"q":"Which fixed phrase expresses 'would like to', and which part of it changes with the person?","a":"gustaría + infinitive, with the indirect object pronoun me/te/le changing for 1st/2nd/3rd person singular."}]},"room":{"facts":["Verbal negation places no before the verb, with nunca, nadie or ninguno either following the verb or replacing no before it.","Present, present continuous, preterite, present perfect and periphrastic future are required across all six persons; imperfect, imperfect continuous, future, conditional and the imperative are required for 1st-3rd singular only (imperative: tú only).","Reflexive verbs are used in the singular only; a reflexive form with the same meaning as its base verb is not listed separately, one with a different meaning is listed and only tested if listed.","Comparatives: menos...que (less … than) is the structure listed for adjectives, with irregular mejor/peor (Esta película es menos interesante que esa); adverbs compare with más...que/de, menos...que/de or tan...como, sharing mejor/peor (Corre más rápidamente que yo).","Modals (deber, poder, querer, tener que, saber) + infinitive are required across all six persons; the two fixed phrases quisiera + infinitive and me/te/le gustaría + infinitive are listed within the same modal entry.","-ito/-ita ('little'/affection) and -ísimo/-ísima ('very') always change meaning the same way; -mente, -idad and -able change the part of speech, but only count when the English equivalent is '-ly', '-(i)ty' or '-able'."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I am looking for my brother in the park.'","a":"Busco a mi hermano en el parque.","sol":"Busco a mi hermano en el parque. 'my brother' is a specific person as the direct object, so the personal a goes before him; 'in the park' uses en with the article; buscar already means 'to look for', so no extra preposition follows it.","hints":["One of the objects in this sentence is a specific person — what does that add in Spanish?","Does 'look for' need its own preposition in Spanish, and which preposition gives 'in'?","Verb + person phrase + place phrase."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Prepositions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Where do you (tú) live?'","a":"¿Dónde vives?","sol":"¿Dónde vives? — the wh-word dónde is followed by the verb vives (2nd person singular); no subject pronoun is needed since tú is understood from the verb form.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["What is the Spanish word for 'where'?","What is 'to live' in the tú form?","Put the question marks and both words in the right order."],"codes":["Interrogatives"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the impersonal se: 'You can recycle glass.'","a":"Se puede reciclar el vidrio.","sol":"Se puede reciclar el vidrio. — se puede + infinitive gives the generalised 'you/one can' meaning, followed by the infinitive reciclar and the object.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal construction gives a generalised 'you/one can' meaning?","What form does the main verb take after se puede?","Put the impersonal phrase, the infinitive and the object in order."],"codes":["Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the personal a: 'I visit my grandmother on Sundays.'","a":"Visito a mi abuela los domingos.","sol":"Visito a mi abuela los domingos. 'my grandmother' is a specific person as the direct object, so the personal a goes before her; 'on Sundays' is los domingos with no preposition.","hints":["What extra word is needed before a specific person used as a direct object?","What is 'to visit' in the yo form?","Verb, the person phrase, then the day phrase."],"m":2,"d":1,"codes":["Prepositions"]},{"q":"Choose the correct form for each gap from the three given: Ayer (yo) ___ mis deberes (hago / hice / hacía); mi madre ___ que es fácil (pensa / piensa / pienso); ahora (yo) ___ mucha hambre (teno / tengo / tiene).","a":"hice; piensa; tengo","sol":"hice — the irregular preterite stem of hacer in the yo form, listed in the vocabulary list (not a regular -é/-í ending); piensa — pensar changes its stem e→ie in the stressed forms; tengo — the yo form of tener adds g, and tener hambre expresses a state where English says \"to be hungry\".","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One gap needs a finished past action; the other two are present-tense forms with a change inside the stem.","For the present-tense gaps, check which option shows the stem change the verb takes in that person.","Three verb forms, in order, separated by semicolons."],"codes":["Inflectional morphology"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the correct word order for an interesar-type verb: 'We are interested in music.'","a":"Nos interesa la música.","sol":"Nos interesa la música. — nos (indirect object, 'us') comes before the verb, and la música (singular) is the grammatical subject controlling the singular verb interesa.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Which pronoun expresses 'us' as the person interested?","Is the thing that interests them singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb?","Put the pronoun, verb and subject in Object-Verb-Subject order."],"codes":["Syntax of interesar-type verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using an impersonal structure: 'One needs a ticket to enter.'","a":"Se necesita una entrada para entrar.","sol":"Se necesita una entrada para entrar. 'One needs' with no particular person is the impersonal se + verb, agreeing with the singular noun that follows; 'to enter' as a purpose is para + infinitive.","hints":["Nobody in particular is doing the needing — which impersonal pattern fits?","Is the thing needed singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb?","Impersonal verb phrase + noun + purpose phrase with an infinitive."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using a reflexive verb in the correct person: 'She gets up at seven.'","a":"Se levanta a las siete.","sol":"Se levanta a las siete. — levantarse, 3rd person singular, se levanta, followed by the time phrase a las siete.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Which reflexive verb means 'to get up'?","What form does it take for 'she'?","Put the reflexive verb and the time phrase in order."],"codes":["Reflexive use of verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, with the adjective agreeing correctly: 'The girls are very tall.'","a":"Las chicas son muy altas.","sol":"Las chicas son muy altas. — altas agrees in gender and number with the feminine plural noun chicas, and ser is used since height is a lasting trait.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["What gender and number is chicas, and how must the adjective agree?","Which verb, ser or estar, is used for a lasting characteristic?","Put the subject, verb, intensifier and adjective in order."],"codes":["Adjectival phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using an adverbial comparative: 'My sister speaks more slowly than me.'","a":"Mi hermana habla más despacio que yo.","sol":"Mi hermana habla más despacio que yo. — más...que is the adverbial comparative for 'more...than', and yo (not mí) follows que.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which comparative structure expresses 'more...than' with an adverb?","What pronoun follows que when comparing with 'me'?","Put the subject, verb, comparative and pronoun in order."],"codes":["Adverbial phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, placing each adverb where it normally goes: 'I always do my homework quickly at home.'","a":"Siempre hago los deberes rápidamente en casa.","sol":"Siempre hago los deberes rápidamente en casa. The adverb of time (siempre) opens the sentence or sits before the verb; the adverb of manner (rápidamente) follows the verb and its object; the phrase of place (en casa) comes at the end.","hints":["Sort the three adverbial elements into time, manner and place before you place any of them.","Which of the three normally goes before the verb, and which two follow the verb and its object?","One sentence containing a time word, the verb with its object, a manner adverb and a place phrase."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Adverbial phrases"]},{"q":"Explain what facilísimo means and exactly how it is formed from fácil.","a":"'Extremely/very easy' — formed by adding -ísimo directly to fácil (dropping its written accent, since the word is no longer stressed on that syllable), since fácil ends in a consonant so no letter is removed.","sol":"facilísimo means 'extremely easy' or 'very easy'. Because fácil ends in a consonant rather than -o or -a, no letter is removed before -ísimo is added: fácil + ísimo = facilísimo — but the written accent on fácil is dropped, since facilísimo is now stressed on the -í- of -ísimo, not on the original syllable.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Does fácil end in -o, -a, or something else, and does that change how -ísimo is added?","What does the -ísimo ending always mean when added to an adjective?","Give both the meaning and the exact formation."],"codes":["Uniformly applicable derivational morphology that changes meaning"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Por tu seguridad, el peligro es evitable si sigues las reglas.'","a":"For your safety, the danger is avoidable if you follow the rules.","sol":"Por tu seguridad, el peligro es evitable si sigues las reglas — For your safety, the danger is avoidable if you follow the rules. seguridad is -idad added to seguro (minus its final vowel); evitable is -able added to the verb stem evitar.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The first noun is built from an adjective plus a noun-forming suffix — which adjective?","The adjective after es is built from a verb plus a suffix that turns it into an adjective.","Give one complete English sentence."],"codes":["Other high frequency patterns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I would like to visit Spain next summer because I am interested in Spanish culture. I have to save money, so I don't buy many things. I never eat fast food because it isn't healthy.'","a":"Me gustaría visitar España el verano que viene porque me interesa la cultura española. Tengo que ahorrar dinero, así que no compro muchas cosas. Nunca como comida rápida porque no es saludable.","sol":"Me gustaría visitar España el verano que viene porque me interesa la cultura española. Tengo que ahorrar dinero, así que no compro muchas cosas. Nunca como comida rápida porque no es saludable. (Uses gustaría + infinitive, the interesar Object-Verb-Subject pattern, tener que + infinitive, and nunca placed before the verb.)","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Which fixed phrase expresses 'I would like to', and which pattern does 'I am interested in' follow?","Which modal expresses 'I have to', and where does the word for 'never' go in the last sentence?","Three sentences: fixed phrase + infinitive + reason; modal + infinitive, then a consequence clause with a negative; negative word + verb + reason."],"codes":["Modals, for 1st, 2nd and 3rd persons in singular and plural","Syntax of interesar-type verbs","Negation"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Mi hermano nunca hace sus deberes porque dice que no tiene tiempo, pero le interesan mucho los videojuegos.' Answer in English: what does the brother never do, and what interests him a lot?","a":"He never does his homework; video games interest him a lot.","sol":"Mi hermano nunca hace sus deberes — my brother never does his homework; le interesan mucho los videojuegos — video games interest him a lot (interesar, Object-Verb-Subject order, agreeing with the plural videojuegos).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the negative word and what it says the brother never does.","Find the interesar-type verb and what it says interests him.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Negation","Syntax of interesar-type verbs"]},{"q":"Write about 50 words in Spanish describing your daily routine, including a reflexive verb, a negative sentence with nunca or nadie, and an opinion using an interesar-type verb.","a":"e.g. Todos los días me levanto a las siete y desayuno rápido. Nunca desayuno mucho porque no tengo tiempo. Me interesan mucho los deportes, así que hago ejercicio antes del colegio. Por la tarde, no hablo con nadie porque estoy muy cansado, y me acuesto temprano.","sol":"Todos los días me levanto a las siete y desayuno rápido. Nunca desayuno mucho porque no tengo tiempo. Me interesan mucho los deportes, así que hago ejercicio antes del colegio. Por la tarde, no hablo con nadie porque estoy muy cansado, y me acuesto temprano. (45 words: me levanto/me acuesto are reflexive, nunca desayuno and no hablo con nadie are negation, me interesan is the interesar-type opinion.)","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Which verb type describes an action you do to yourself, such as what you do first in the morning?","Use a reflexive verb for at least one daily activity, then one negative sentence, then an opinion.","About 50 words in four or five sentences: the routine, the negative sentence, then the opinion."],"codes":["Reflexive use of verbs","Negation","Syntax of interesar-type verbs"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Translate: 'Nunca voy al gimnasio los domingos.'","a":"I never go to the gym on Sundays."},{"q":"What preposition is required before a specific person used as a direct object?","a":"The personal a."},{"q":"What does adding -mente to the feminine singular form of an adjective create, and what does it mean?","a":"An adverb, meaning '-ly'."},{"q":"Translate: 'Quisiera pedir un café, por favor.'","a":"I would like to order a coffee, please."}]},"cards":[{"front":"nunca","back":"never","code":"Negation","open":false},{"front":"levantarse","back":"to get up. Me levanto a las siete. (I get up at seven.)","code":"Reflexive use of verbs","open":false},{"front":"¿dónde?","back":"where?","code":"Interrogatives","open":false},{"front":"me interesa(n)","back":"I am interested in … (the verb agrees with the thing). Me interesan los deportes.","code":"Syntax of interesar-type verbs","open":false},{"front":"hay que + infinitivo","back":"it is necessary to + verb","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"se puede + infinitivo","back":"you can / one can + verb","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"menos...que","back":"less...than. Esta película es menos interesante que esa. (This film is less interesting than that one.)","code":"Adjectival phrases","open":false},{"front":"más...que","back":"more...than. Mi hermano corre más rápidamente que yo. (My brother runs faster than me.)","code":"Adverbial phrases","open":false},{"front":"ver a alguien","back":"to see someone (personal a before a specific person). Veo a mi madre.","code":"Prepositions","open":false},{"front":"facilísimo","back":"extremely easy (fácil + -ísimo)","code":"Uniformly applicable derivational morphology that changes meaning","open":false},{"front":"librito","back":"little book (libro + -ito)","code":"Derivational morphology (Reading only)","open":false},{"front":"rápidamente","back":"quickly (rápida + -mente)","code":"Other high frequency patterns","open":false},{"front":"seguridad","back":"safety, security (seguro + -idad)","code":"Other high frequency patterns","open":false},{"front":"tener que + infinitivo","back":"to have to + verb","code":"Modals, for 1st, 2nd and 3rd persons in singular and plural","open":false},{"front":"hice","back":"I did, I made (irregular preterite of hacer)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: negación, interrogativos e impersonales","items":["no - not/no","nunca - never","nadie - nobody/anybody","ninguno/a - none/not any","qué - what","quién - who","cuándo - when","por qué - why","cómo - how","cuál - which","dónde - where","hay - there is/are","hay que - it is necessary to","se puede - you can/one can","se necesita - one needs","hace sol - it's sunny","hace frío - it's cold","buscar - to look for","los deberes - homework","ahorrar - to save (money)","el vidrio - glass","la entrada - ticket"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: adjetivos, adverbios y comparativos","items":["interesante - interesting","alto/a - tall","mejor - better","peor - worse","único/a - unique/only (meaning changes with position)","rápidamente - quickly","despacio - slowly","bien - well","mal - badly","aquí - here","allí - there","tan...como - as...as","levantarse - to get up","los videojuegos - video games","la comida rápida - fast food","saludable - healthy"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: derivación (-ito, -ísimo, -mente, -idad, -able)","items":["librito - little book","hermanito - little brother","facilísimo - extremely easy","guapísimo - extremely good-looking","normalmente - normally","absolutamente - absolutely","seguridad - security/safety","realidad - reality","evitable - avoidable","aceptable - acceptable"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: frases verbales, adjetivas y adverbiales","items":["Negación: no + verbo, con nunca/nadie/ninguno después (o antes del verbo, sin 'no'). No como nunca carne. / Nunca como carne. (I never eat meat.)","Interrogativos: palabra interrogativa + verbo + sujeto (si se expresa). ¿Dónde vive Ana? (Where does Ana live?)","Interesar-type: pronombre de objeto indirecto + verbo + sujeto (orden inverso al inglés). Me interesan los deportes. (I am interested in sport.)","Modales + infinitivo (las seis personas): deber, poder, querer, tener que, saber. Tienen que estudiar. (They have to study.)","Frases fijas dentro de los modales: quisiera + infinitivo; me/te/le gustaría + infinitivo. Le gustaría viajar a España. (He/She would like to travel to Spain.)","Reflexivos (solo singular): me levanto, te llamas, se ducha. Me levanto a las siete. (I get up at seven.)","Comparativo con adjetivos: menos...que / más...que (+ mejor/peor). Esta película es menos interesante que esa. (This film is less interesting than that one.)","Comparativo con adverbios: más/menos...que, tan...como (+ mejor/peor). Mi hermano corre más rápidamente que yo. (My brother runs faster than me.)","A personal: delante de una persona concreta como objeto directo. Busco a mi madre en la tienda. (I am looking for my mother in the shop.)","Derivación en lectura: la forma derivada puede aparecer en un texto aunque solo se liste la base (libro → librito). Mi abuela me regaló un librito. (My grandmother gave me a little book.)","Cambios de raíz: o→ue (puedo), e→ie (pienso), e→i (pido), c→zc (conozco), +g (pongo). Pienso que puedo hacerlo. (I think I can do it.)","tener + sustantivo para estados: tener frío / hambre / años. Tengo hambre y mi hermano tiene frío. (I am hungry and my brother is cold.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.541Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the Spanish is sound throughout, but the kit over-tests a handful of easy structures (personal a, comparatives) while leaving most of the huge 'Inflectional morphology' idea untested, and it recycles the same two comparative sentences across examples, facts, cards and questions."}}},"3.2.2.1":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.2.1","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier noun phrases add two things to what Foundation already covers: the demonstrative aquel (and its neuter and pronoun forms) for pointing at something further away, and a set of pronoun forms — object pronoun word order, possessive pronouns, relative pronouns and pronouns after prepositions — that Paper 4's translation and writing tasks reward when they appear correctly, and that Papers 1 and 3 test you can recognise when reading or listening. Marks are lost less often for not knowing a rule exists than for using el que where a subject relative clause is needed, or writing el mío when the sentence actually needs mío as an adjective, so this room drills the exact distinctions the specification names.","idea":[{"code":"Determiners","h":"aquel: the third 'that/those', further away","t":"Spanish has three distances: este (this, near me), ese (that, near you), and aquel (that, over there — away from both of us). Aquel agrees like an -o/-a adjective: aquel chico, aquella chica, aquellos chicos, aquellas chicas. It sits before the noun: Aquellas montañas al fondo son muy altas (Those mountains in the background are very tall). You are allowed to use ese instead of aquel in your own writing and speaking without losing marks, but you must still recognise aquel, aquella, aquellos and aquellas when you meet them in a reading or listening text, and know that they mean something further away than ese."},{"code":"Pronouns","h":"Object pronoun order, possessives, relatives and pronouns after a preposition","t":"Object pronouns nos (us) and os (you, plural informal) work like all the others: before a conjugated verb, or attached to an infinitive — Os veo (I see you) / Os puedo ver or Puedo veros (I can see you); with two verbs, either position is correct. The same pronouns are also plural reflexives: Nos levantamos a las siete (We get up at seven); Os laváis las manos (You wash your hands); when the reflexive shows something done to each other rather than to yourself, it is reciprocal: Nos vemos todos los días (We see each other every day) — this reciprocal reading only makes sense for a plural subject. Possessive pronouns replace a noun already understood: el mío, la mía, el tuyo, el suyo, el nuestro, el vuestro (mine, yours, his/hers/theirs, ours, yours-plural), agreeing with the thing owned, not the owner — Mi coche es azul; el tuyo es rojo (My car is blue; yours is red). Keep this apart from the possessive adjective, which sits with a noun: mi coche (my car) uses mi as an adjective, whereas el mío means 'mine' on its own with no noun following. lo que (what, that which) never changes; it introduces what you don't yet know: No entiendo lo que dice (I don't understand what he's saying). el que, la que, los que, las que and el cual, la cual, los cuales, las cuales introduce a subject relative clause — the noun they stand for is the subject of the verb that follows. Only the el que set can stand on its own with no noun before it, meaning 'the one(s) who / whoever': El que estudia más saca mejores notas (The one who studies most gets the best marks). Both sets can pick out one of several people already mentioned, and here el cual is the more formal choice: Mi amiga, la que (or la cual) se sienta a mi lado, me ayudó (My friend, the one who sits next to me, helped me) — el cual always needs a stated noun in front of it. With a simple stated noun, que alone is the usual choice: El libro que leí ayer era interesante (The book that I read yesterday was interesting). The neuter pronoun aquello refers to a whole idea rather than one noun: Aquello no me gustó nada (I didn't like that at all). Other relative words keep their normal meaning: cuando (when), donde (where), que (that/which/who). After a preposition, the pronouns mí, ti, él, ella, usted, nosotros, vosotros, ellos/ellas replace a noun — para mí, con ella — except that con + mí and con + ti fuse into conmigo and contigo (¿Vienes conmigo? — Are you coming with me?). Adding a mí, a ti and so on after the verb is emphatic, not the object itself: A mí me gusta el fútbol (I really like football, emphasising 'I')."}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing esta or aquella from a described scene","setup":"You are describing a photo. There are two mountains: one is right in the foreground, close to the church; the other is a small shape right at the back of the picture. Complete: '___ montaña, cerca de la iglesia, es más verde que ___ montaña al fondo.'","steps":["The first mountain is right in the foreground, the part of the photo closest to the describer, so it takes the nearest distance: Esta montaña.","The second mountain is described as being right at the back — the furthest point in the picture — so it takes the furthest distance: aquella montaña.","Check agreement: montaña is feminine singular, so both determiners must be feminine singular too: esta, aquella.","Complete sentence: Esta montaña, cerca de la iglesia, es más verde que aquella montaña al fondo."],"cues":["Where in the picture is each mountain, according to the description?","Which mountain is described as furthest away, and which determiner marks that distance?","What gender and number is montaña, and what does that do to both determiners?","What do you get when you put both determiners into the full sentence?"]},{"title":"Object pronoun position with a modal verb","setup":"Translate this exchange into Spanish, giving both correct word orders for the second line: 'Can you help us?' 'Yes, I can help you (plural).'","steps":["The first line has one conjugated verb (puedes) and one infinitive (ayudar); the object is nos, so it goes either before puedes or attached to ayudar: ¿Nos puedes ayudar? or ¿Puedes ayudarnos?","The reply also has a modal plus infinitive (puedo ayudar), and the object this time is os, giving the same two options.","Attach os to the infinitive: Sí, puedo ayudaros.","Or place it before the conjugated verb instead, giving the second correct answer: Sí, os puedo ayudar."],"cues":["How many verbs are in 'can you help us', and which one is conjugated?","In a two-verb construction, where can the object pronoun go?","Attach os to ayudar first: what does that give you?","Now move the same pronoun in front of the conjugated verb instead: what changes?"]},{"title":"Headless el que versus a possessive pronoun","setup":"Translate: 'The one who arrives first has a car; mine is older than his.'","steps":["'The one who arrives first' names no specific noun before it, so it needs the headless subject relative: El que llega primero.","'has a car' completes that first clause: tiene un coche.","'Mine' stands in for coche with no noun following, so it needs the possessive pronoun, not the adjective: el mío.","'His' also stands alone for coche, agreeing with coche (masculine), not with the owner: el suyo. Full sentence: El que llega primero tiene un coche; el mío es más viejo que el suyo."],"cues":["Is there a specific noun named before 'who arrives first', or does the sentence start straight with it?","What completes that first clause?","Does 'mine' have a noun straight after it in the English, or does it stand on its own?","What does 'his' agree with here: the owner, or the thing owned?"]},{"title":"Reciprocal reflexive with nos","setup":"Translate: 'My best friend and I write to each other every week.' (Use escribirse.)","steps":["The subject is plural (mi mejor amigo y yo = nosotros), so the reflexive pronoun must be the plural form, nos.","Conjugate escribir for nosotros: escribimos.","Because the two people write to each other, not to themselves, nos here means 'each other' (reciprocal), so no extra word for 'each other' is needed: nos escribimos.","Add the time phrase: Mi mejor amigo y yo nos escribimos todas las semanas."],"cues":["Is the subject of this sentence singular or plural?","What is the nosotros form of escribir?","With a plural subject, can nos mean 'each other' as well as 'ourselves'?","Put the pronoun, verb and time phrase together in the right order."]}],"check":[{"q":"When can you use ese instead of aquel without losing marks?","a":"In your own writing and speaking (production) — but you must still recognise aquel/aquella/aquellos/aquellas and know they mean 'further away' when reading or listening."},{"q":"What is the difference between mi coche and el mío?","a":"mi coche uses the possessive adjective mi with the noun coche stated; el mío is the possessive pronoun standing in for coche, with no noun following."},{"q":"In 'Mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me ayudó', what does la que do?","a":"It picks out which friend is meant — 'the one who sits next to me' — as the subject of se sienta."},{"q":"What does conmigo mean, and how is it formed?","a":"'With me' — it is the fused form of con + mí, used instead of con mí."}]},"room":{"facts":["Three distances: este/esta (this), ese/esa (that, near you), aquel/aquella (that, further away) — aquellos/aquellas in the plural.","el mío/la mía/el tuyo/el suyo/el nuestro/el vuestro are possessive pronouns (stand alone, no noun follows); mi/tu/su etc with a noun are possessive adjectives (mi coche).","el que/la que/los que/las que introduce a subject relative clause and can stand alone, meaning 'the one(s) who/that' (El que estudia más saca mejores notas); el cual/la cual/los cuales/las cuales do the same job only after a stated noun (mi amiga, la cual se sienta a mi lado) and never stand alone; with a simple stated noun, que alone is the usual choice.","lo que never changes for gender or number; aquello is the neuter pronoun for a whole idea, not one noun.","con + mí = conmigo; con + ti = contigo. Other prepositional pronouns (para mí, con ella, a nosotros) keep their normal form.","nos and os, with a plural subject, can mean 'each other' (reciprocal) as well as 'ourselves/yourselves'."],"questions":[{"q":"Complete with the correct form of aquel: '___ edificio al fondo de la calle es el ayuntamiento.' (edificio = building)","a":"Aquel","sol":"Aquel edificio al fondo de la calle es el ayuntamiento. Edificio is masculine singular, and 'at the end of the street' signals the furthest distance, so aquel (not ese) is needed.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decide the gender and number of edificio before choosing a form.","Where in the street is this building — close to the speaker, or far away?","Give one word only."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Complete with the correct form of aquel: 'Aquellos zapatos son más baratos que ___ botas.' (zapatos = shoes, m; botas = boots, f)","a":"aquellas","sol":"Aquellos zapatos son más baratos que aquellas botas. Botas is feminine plural, so the second determiner must match it: aquellas, not the masculine aquellos already used for zapatos.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The first determiner is already given — look at what it agrees with.","Check the gender and number of botas, not zapatos.","Give one word only."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Choose the correct word for the gap: '___ estudia más saca mejores notas.' (a) El que (b) Lo que (c) Aquello","a":"El que","sol":"El que estudia más saca mejores notas. El que stands for 'the one who / whoever' — a person, a specific kind of referent — so el que is correct; lo que would only fit for an abstract idea, not a person.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Is the gap standing in for a person, or for an abstract idea?","Does the subject of estudia have a specific kind of referent — a person — or none at all?","Write out your choice in full rather than giving a letter."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The girl who lives next door is my friend.'","a":"La chica que vive al lado es mi amiga.","sol":"La chica que vive al lado es mi amiga. 'Who lives next door' describes chica, the subject of vive, so a subject relative is needed; que alone is what the specification tests for a relative clause attached to a stated noun like this.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the noun that 'who lives next door' is describing.","Is that noun the subject of the verb that follows it?","One clause, then mi amiga at the end."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My sister's bag is red; mine is black.'","a":"El bolso de mi hermana es rojo; el mío es negro.","sol":"El bolso de mi hermana es rojo; el mío es negro. 'Mine' stands alone for bolso (masculine) with no noun following it, so the possessive pronoun el mío is needed, not the adjective mío on its own.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Decide whether 'mine' has a noun straight after it in English.","The possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, not with the speaker.","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Rewrite, moving the object pronoun to the other correct position: 'Quiero veros mañana.'","a":"Os quiero ver mañana.","sol":"Os quiero ver mañana. With a conjugated verb (quiero) plus an infinitive (ver), the plural object pronoun os may attach to the infinitive (veros) or move in front of the conjugated verb (os quiero); both are correct, and the task asks for the other one.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the two verbs in the sentence: which is conjugated and which is the infinitive?","The pronoun can go in front of the conjugated verb instead of attached to the infinitive.","Move only the pronoun; keep mañana at the end."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using a reciprocal reflexive: 'My cousins see each other every summer.'","a":"Mis primos se ven todos los veranos.","sol":"Mis primos se ven todos los veranos. The subject (mis primos) is plural, so the third-person plural reflexive se is used with the reciprocal meaning 'each other'; ver conjugates regularly to ven for ellos.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which reflexive pronoun goes with a third-person plural subject?","With a plural subject, this reflexive can mean 'each other'.","Verb, then the time phrase, in that order."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'No entendí lo que dijo el profesor, pero mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me lo explicó.'","a":"I didn't understand what the teacher said, but my friend, the one who sits next to me, explained it to me.","sol":"No entendí lo que dijo el profesor, pero mi amiga, la que se sienta a mi lado, me lo explicó. lo que stands for the whole idea of what the teacher said; la que here picks out a specific friend already named, as the subject of se sienta.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["lo que refers to a whole idea, not one noun — what does the teacher's speech mean here?","la que picks out a specific person already named — who?","Give one complete English sentence."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Are you coming with me? No, I'm going with her.'","a":"¿Vienes conmigo? No, voy con ella.","sol":"¿Vienes conmigo? No, voy con ella. Con + mí fuses to conmigo; ella is unaffected by con and keeps its normal prepositional pronoun form.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["con + mí does not stay as two words — what does it become?","Does con fuse with every pronoun, or only mí and ti?","Two short sentences, the second starting with No."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, choosing the correct possessive forms: 'My phone is old; yours is new.'","a":"Mi móvil es viejo; el tuyo es nuevo.","sol":"Mi móvil es viejo; el tuyo es nuevo. The first clause names móvil directly, using mi as a possessive adjective; the second clause does not repeat móvil, so the full possessive pronoun el tuyo is needed instead.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first clause names the phone directly, using a possessive adjective.","The second clause doesn't repeat 'phone' — which form fits when the noun isn't restated?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Complete with aquel or ese as appropriate, giving a reason: 'Prefiero ___ camisa que llevas puesta ahora mismo.' (the shirt the listener is wearing right now)","a":"esa — the shirt is close to the listener (they are wearing it), so the middle-distance form fits, not aquella.","sol":"Prefiero esa camisa que llevas puesta ahora mismo. The shirt belongs to the person being spoken to and is close to them, so the middle distance ese/esa is correct, not aquella, which would describe something further from both speakers.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Whose shirt is it, and how close is it to the listener?","Which of the two distances fits something the listener is holding or wearing right now?","Give the form and a short reason about distance."],"codes":["Determiners"]},{"q":"Write two Spanish sentences about a family photo, each correctly using one possessive pronoun (el mío/la mía, el tuyo/la tuya, el suyo/la suya, el nuestro/la nuestra or el vuestro/la vuestra).","a":"Two sentences, e.g. 'Mi madre está a la izquierda y la tuya a la derecha.' / 'Mi hermano es más alto que el tuyo.'","sol":"Example: En la foto, mi madre está a la izquierda y la tuya está a la derecha. Mi hermano es más alto que el tuyo. Each sentence uses one possessive pronoun (la tuya agreeing with madre; el tuyo agreeing with hermano), standing alone with no noun repeated after it, with a consistent tú addressee.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Name the noun each possessive pronoun will stand in for before you write anything.","Check every possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned, not the owner.","Two linked sentences, not a list."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Aquella casa al final de la calle es la nuestra; la tuya es la que tiene el jardín grande, ¿verdad?' Answer in English: which house belongs to the speaker's family (ours), and how is the listener's house identified?","a":"The speaker's family's house (ours) is the one at the end of the street, further away; the listener's is the one with the big garden.","sol":"Aquella casa al final de la calle es la nuestra — that house (over there) at the end of the street is ours; la tuya es la que tiene el jardín grande — yours is the one that has the big garden. aquella marks the far distance; la nuestra and la tuya are possessive pronouns standing for casa; la que introduces the subject relative clause identifying the listener's house.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the phrase saying which house is 'ours', and where it is.","Find the phrase describing the listener's house.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Determiners","Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My cousins write letters to each other every month; theirs are longer than mine.'","a":"Mis primos se escriben cartas cada mes; las suyas son más largas que las mías.","sol":"Mis primos se escriben cartas cada mes; las suyas son más largas que las mías. se escriben cartas is a reciprocal reflexive ('write to each other'); 'theirs' and 'mine' both stand for cartas (feminine plural), so both take the possessive pronoun agreeing with cartas: las suyas, las mías.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Which reflexive pronoun and verb form express 'write to each other' for a plural subject?","What plural feminine noun are 'theirs' and 'mine' both standing in for?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon, with both possessive pronouns agreeing with that noun."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using relative pronouns: 'That is the town where I was born, and I was very happy when I lived there.'","a":"Ese es el pueblo donde nací, y era muy feliz cuando vivía allí.","sol":"Ese es el pueblo donde nací, y era muy feliz cuando vivía allí. donde introduces a relative clause of place; cuando introduces a temporal clause, both keeping their normal meaning as relative words.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which relative word means 'where', introducing the first clause?","Which word means 'when', introducing the second clause as an ordinary temporal clause?","Two linked clauses in one sentence."],"codes":["Pronouns"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the emphatic form: 'I really love the mountains; that surprised my friend, who prefers the beach.'","a":"A mí me encantan las montañas; aquello sorprendió a mi amigo, que prefiere la playa.","sol":"A mí me encantan las montañas; aquello sorprendió a mi amigo, que prefiere la playa. A mí adds emphasis to me encantan; aquello is the neuter pronoun referring to the whole situation just described, not one specific noun.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Which extra phrase, before me, adds emphasis without changing the meaning?","Which neuter pronoun refers to the whole idea just mentioned, rather than one noun?","Two clauses joined by a semicolon."],"codes":["Pronouns"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the four forms of aquel (m/f, singular/plural).","a":"aquel, aquella, aquellos, aquellas"},{"q":"What does el mío mean, and what part of speech is it?","a":"'Mine' — a possessive pronoun, standing alone with no noun following."},{"q":"What does el que mean in 'El que estudia más saca mejores notas'?","a":"'The one who' / 'whoever' — a subject relative pronoun standing for the person who studies."},{"q":"What is the fused form of con + ti?","a":"contigo"}]},"cards":[{"front":"aquella montaña","back":"that mountain (over there, further than ese). Aquella montaña al fondo es muy alta. (That mountain in the background is very tall.)","code":"Determiners","open":false},{"front":"aquel edificio","back":"that building (over there). Aquel edificio es el ayuntamiento. (That building is the town hall.)","code":"Determiners","open":false},{"front":"el mío / la mía","back":"mine — possessive pronoun, stands alone. Este bolso no es tuyo; es el mío. (This bag isn't yours; it's mine.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el tuyo / el suyo","back":"yours / his-hers-theirs — possessive pronoun, agrees with the thing owned.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el nuestro / el vuestro","back":"ours / yours (plural) — possessive pronoun, agrees with the thing owned.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"el que estudia más","back":"the one who studies most — headless subject relative. El que estudia más saca mejores notas. (The one who studies most gets the best marks.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"lo que dice","back":"what he/she says — lo que is invariable, used for an idea rather than one noun.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"aquello","back":"that (neuter pronoun) — refers to a whole idea, not one noun. Aquello no me gustó nada. (I didn't like that at all.)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"conmigo / contigo","back":"with me / with you — fused forms of con + mí / con + ti. ¿Vienes conmigo? (Are you coming with me?)","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"a mí me gusta","back":"I really like (emphatic) — a mí adds emphasis without changing the meaning of me gusta.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"nos vemos","back":"we see each other — reciprocal reflexive, plural subject only.","code":"Pronouns","open":false},{"front":"puedo veros / os puedo ver","back":"I can see you (plural) — nos and os go before the conjugated verb, or attach to the infinitive; both are correct.","code":"Pronouns","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: los determinantes aquel","items":["aquel (m sing) - that (over there)","aquella (f sing) - that (over there)","aquellos (m pl) - those (over there)","aquellas (f pl) - those (over there)","al fondo - in the background, at the far end","allí / allá - over there","aquello - that (neuter, a whole idea)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: los pronombres posesivos y relativos","items":["el mío / la mía / los míos / las mías - mine","el tuyo / la tuya / los tuyos / las tuyas - yours (informal singular)","el suyo / la suya / los suyos / las suyas - his / hers / yours (formal) / theirs","el nuestro / la nuestra / los nuestros / las nuestras - ours","el vuestro / la vuestra / los vuestros / las vuestras - yours (informal plural)","el que / la que / los que / las que - the one(s) who/that (headless subject relative)","el cual / la cual / los cuales / las cuales - who/which (formal; only after a stated noun)","lo que - what, that which","conmigo - with me","contigo - with you"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: pronombres de nivel superior","items":["Object pronoun with a modal + infinitive: before the conjugated verb or attached to the infinitive, both correct — Nos puede ayudar. / Puede ayudarnos. (He/She can help us.)","Reciprocal reflexive (plural subject only): Nos escribimos cada semana. (We write to each other every week.)","Possessive pronoun agrees with the thing owned: Mi libro es nuevo; el tuyo es viejo. (My book is new; yours is old.)","Possessive adjective (noun stated) vs possessive pronoun (no noun): mi coche / el mío. Example: Mi coche es viejo, pero el tuyo es nuevo. (My car is old, but yours is new.)","el que como relativo sin antecedente ('el que' = quien); el cual solo después de un sustantivo. El que llega tarde no puede entrar. (Whoever arrives late cannot come in.) Mi vecina, la cual trabaja en el hospital, llega tarde. (My neighbour, who works at the hospital, arrives late.)","lo que for an unspecified idea: Lo que más me gusta es viajar. (What I like most is travelling.)","Pronoun after a preposition: para mí, con ella, a nosotros — except conmigo, contigo."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.541Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the grammar is sound throughout; fix the answer-revealing hint on the 'girl who lives next door' translation, the aquello/aquel placement in the why-section and vocab list, and the give-away title on the first worked example, and this is publishable."}}},"3.2.2.2":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.2.2","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier verb phrases add the grammar that lets you talk about hypothetical situations, wishes, ongoing actions and passive events — exactly the range Paper 4's higher-mark writing tasks and Paper 1/3's harder texts reward. Getting the subjunctive, the passive and the various time-related multi-verb structures right, rather than avoiding them, is often what separates the higher writing bands from the lower ones.","idea":[{"code":"Negation","h":"ya no, tampoco, ni…ni","t":"ya no means 'no longer': Ya no vivo en Madrid (I no longer live in Madrid). (no) tampoco means 'not … either' / 'neither': No me gusta el fútbol, y a mi hermano tampoco (I don't like football, and neither does my brother). (no) … ni … ni … means 'neither … nor', used for emphasis: No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para viajar (I have neither time nor money to travel). All of these keep the normal Spanish double-negative pattern, with no before the verb when the negative word follows it."},{"code":"Inflectional morphology","h":"Trickier verb forms: plurals, spelling changes, the imperative and the present subjunctive","t":"The future, conditional and imperfect all need their plural forms too, not just singular: hablaremos/hablaréis/hablarán (future); hablaríamos/hablaríais/hablarían (conditional); the irregular imperfect verbs ir, ser and ver also have plural forms (íbamos, erais, veían), used for both a habitual 'used to' meaning and an ongoing 'was/were -ing' meaning. The future and conditional also share five irregular stems, now credit-bearing in the plural too: saber (sabr-), querer (querr-), venir (vendr-), decir (dir-), salir (saldr-) — sabremos, querría, vendréis, dirían, saldríamos. Some verbs change their spelling to keep the same sound, and this is now credit-bearing: sacar → saqué (c→qu before e), coger → cojo (present tense g→j), llegar → llegué (g→gu). Certain -ir verbs change their stem in the third person of the preterite: pedir → pidió/pidieron (e→i), dormir → durmió/durmieron (o→u). The imperative gives direct commands in the tú and vosotros forms: ¡Habla! (Speak!, tú), ¡Hablad! (Speak!, vosotros). The present subjunctive is required, for singular persons only, for five specific verbs — hacer, ser, ir, venir, tener — after cuando referring to the future (Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré — When I have time, I'll call you), after verbs of wishing, command, request or emotion (Quiero que vengas — I want you to come), after que in certain fixed patterns, and after para que to express purpose (Se lo explico para que tenga tiempo de prepararse — I'm explaining it to her so that she has time to prepare)."},{"code":"Multi-verb expressions","h":"acabar de, the passive, seguir/llevar + gerund, desde hace","t":"acabar de + infinitive means 'to have just done something': Acabo de llegar (I have just arrived). The passive voice is formed with ser + past participle + por: La casa fue construida por mi abuelo (The house was built by my grandfather); an alternative, more common passive uses se + a third-person verb: Se construyó la casa en 1950 (The house was built in 1950). seguir + present participle means 'to keep on doing': Sigo estudiando español (I keep on studying Spanish); llevar + a time period + present participle means 'to have been doing something for [time]': Llevo dos años estudiando español (I have been studying Spanish for two years) — the same idea as desde hace, but built differently: Estudio español desde hace dos años means the same thing."},{"code":"Impersonal verbs","h":"parece, basta, falta, hace falta, vale la pena","t":"These verbs describe a general situation rather than naming who is affected, though they can take an indirect object pronoun to show who: (me) parece que … ([it] seems to me that…); basta con + infinitive (it is enough to…); (me) falta / hace falta + noun or infinitive (I need/lack…, it's necessary to…); vale la pena + infinitive (it's worth…): No vale la pena discutir por esto (It's not worth arguing over this)."},{"code":"Adjectival phrases","h":"lo + adjective, possessives after the verb, and superlative adjectives","t":"lo + a masculine singular adjective turns it into an abstract noun: lo bueno es que… (the good thing is that…); lo más difícil (the hardest part). Possessive adjectives can stand as a complement after a verb like ser, agreeing with the thing owned, not written next to the noun: Este libro es mío (This book is mine — not 'el libro mío'); Estos boletos son nuestros (These tickets are ours). The superlative uses el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjective, with de to mean 'in/of': el estudiante más trabajador de la clase (the hardest-working student in the class); irregular superlatives include el mejor (the best) and el peor (the worst)."},{"code":"Adverbial phrases","h":"Superlative adverbs","t":"Superlative adverb structures compare an action against everyone or everything. The regular way is to extend the comparative: más/menos + adverb + que todos / que nadie: Corre más rápido que nadie (He runs faster than anyone — the fastest); Este equipo juega peor que todos los demás (This team plays worse than all the others — the worst). The irregular forms mejor and peor are used in the same way (juega mejor que nadie), and lo más … posible expresses 'as … as possible': Habla lo más despacio posible (Speak as slowly as possible)."},{"code":"Prepositions","h":"antes de / después de + infinitive, and prepositions in questions","t":"antes de and después de are followed by the infinitive, not a conjugated verb, even when the subject is the same as the main clause: Antes de salir, cierro la puerta con llave (Before leaving, I lock the door); Después de comer, vamos a dar un paseo (After eating, we're going to go for a walk). In questions, the preposition stays with its question word at the front, unlike informal English, which often moves it to the end: ¿Con quién hablas? (Who are you talking with? / With whom are you talking?); ¿De qué hablan? (What are they talking about?)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Negation with ya no and tampoco","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I no longer eat meat, and my sister doesn't eat fish either.'","steps":["'I no longer eat meat' uses ya no before the verb: Ya no como carne.","'my sister' is the new subject of the second clause, so it must be stated: mi hermana — the verb will then be 3rd person singular.","'doesn't eat fish either' uses tampoco directly before the verb, which replaces no entirely (no is only needed if tampoco comes after the verb instead): tampoco come pescado.","Full sentence: Ya no como carne, y mi hermana tampoco come pescado."],"cues":["Which word means 'no longer', and where does it go relative to the verb?","Who is the subject of the second clause?","Which word means 'not … either', and where does it go relative to the verb?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"The present subjunctive after cuando referring to the future","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'When I have more free time, I will learn to play the guitar.'","steps":["This cuando clause refers to a future, not-yet-true situation, so it needs the present subjunctive of tener: tenga.","'more free time' follows: más tiempo libre.","The result clause uses the ordinary future tense, not the subjunctive: aprenderé a tocar la guitarra.","Full sentence: Cuando tenga más tiempo libre, aprenderé a tocar la guitarra."],"cues":["Does cuando here refer to something already happening, or something not yet true?","What follows tenga to say 'more free time'?","Which tense does the main, result clause take — subjunctive too, or the ordinary future?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"The passive with se, and acabar de","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'This bridge was built in 1920, and workers have just finished repairing it.'","steps":["'was built' uses the se-passive with a preterite verb: se construyó.","The bridge is the subject and the year gives the time frame: Este puente se construyó en 1920.","'have just finished repairing it' uses acabar de + infinitive: acaban de terminar de repararlo.","Full sentence: Este puente se construyó en 1920, y los obreros acaban de terminar de repararlo."],"cues":["'was built' does not name who built it — which passive structure fits that?","What is the subject of se construyó, and where does the year go?","Which structure means 'have just done' something?","Write the complete sentence."]},{"title":"Superlative adverb versus comparative","setup":"Translate into Spanish, choosing between a comparative and a superlative: 'Of all my friends, Ana studies the hardest; she studies harder than me too.'","steps":["'studies the hardest' of the whole group compares against everyone else: estudia más que nadie.","'Of all my friends, Ana' opens the sentence: De todos mis amigos, Ana.","'studies harder than me' compares just one other person: estudia más que yo.","Full sentence: De todos mis amigos, Ana estudia más que nadie; también estudia más que yo."],"cues":["Is 'studies the hardest' comparing Ana with one person, or with everyone?","How does the sentence open, naming the whole group?","What follows que when comparing against just one person?","Write both clauses."]}],"check":[{"q":"What does ya no mean, and where does it go relative to the verb?","a":"'No longer' — it goes directly before the verb."},{"q":"After cuando referring to something not yet true (a future condition), which mood is used?","a":"The present subjunctive."},{"q":"What is the difference between más rápido que su hermano and más rápido que nadie?","a":"que + one person/thing is an ordinary comparative ('faster than his brother'); que nadie extends the same comparative to everyone, giving a superlative meaning ('faster than anyone' = the fastest)."},{"q":"What form follows antes de and después de, even with the same subject as the main clause?","a":"The infinitive, not a conjugated verb."}]},"room":{"facts":["ya no = no longer; (no) tampoco = not … either; (no) ni … ni … = neither … nor.","cuando + present subjunctive refers to a future, not-yet-true situation; cuando + present indicative refers to a habitual or current fact.","Passive: ser + past participle + por (names who did it), or se + third-person verb (does not name who).","Adjectival superlative: el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjective + de (of/in a group). Adverbial superlative: más/menos + adverb + que todos / que nadie (juega mejor que nadie), or lo más … posible.","antes de / después de + infinitive, even when the subject doesn't change: Antes de salir, cierro la puerta."],"questions":[{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I no longer play tennis.'","a":"Ya no juego al tenis.","sol":"Ya no juego al tenis. ya no goes directly before the conjugated verb.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which two words mean 'no longer'?","Where do they go relative to the verb?","The sentence is just three words: negative phrase, verb, object."],"codes":["Negation"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'It's worth trying.'","a":"Vale la pena intentarlo.","sol":"Vale la pena intentarlo. vale la pena is followed directly by an infinitive; lo (it) attaches to intentar as its object.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which impersonal phrase means 'it's worth'?","What follows it — a conjugated verb or an infinitive?","Impersonal phrase, then the infinitive with its pronoun attached."],"codes":["Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This car is mine.'","a":"Este coche es mío.","sol":"Este coche es mío. The possessive stands as a complement after ser, agreeing with coche (masculine), not attached to the noun.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Where does the possessive go here — next to the noun, or after the verb ser?","Which form of 'mine' agrees with a masculine noun?","Demonstrative + noun, ser, then the possessive."],"codes":["Adjectival phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using tú commands: 'Speak more slowly and eat your vegetables!'","a":"¡Habla más despacio y come tus verduras!","sol":"¡Habla más despacio y come tus verduras! habla and come are the affirmative tú imperatives of hablar and comer, both regular, matching the 3rd person singular present form.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Which form of the verb gives a direct command to one friend?","Both verbs here are regular in that form.","Two commands joined by y, with exclamation marks."],"codes":["Inflectional morphology"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Before eating, we wash our hands.'","a":"Antes de comer, nos lavamos las manos.","sol":"Antes de comer, nos lavamos las manos. antes de is followed by the infinitive comer, even though the subject (nosotros) is the same in both parts.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["What form does the verb take after antes de, even with the same subject?","'We wash our hands' is a reflexive action — check the verb form carries the right pronoun.","Time clause first, then the reflexive verb and its object."],"codes":["Prepositions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'When you have time, would you like to help me?' (informal singular 'you', referring to a future possibility)","a":"Cuando tengas tiempo, ¿querrías ayudarme?","sol":"Cuando tengas tiempo, ¿querrías ayudarme? cuando refers to a future, not-yet-true moment, so tener takes the present subjunctive (tengas); querrías is the conditional of querer, one of the five irregular conditional stems, for a polite request.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Does cuando here describe something already true, or something that hasn't happened yet?","Which mood does tener take in that case?","Write the complete sentence, keeping the request polite rather than a blunt command."],"codes":["Inflectional morphology"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have been living in this town for six years.'","a":"Vivo en este pueblo desde hace seis años.","sol":"Vivo en este pueblo desde hace seis años. Present tense (vivo) with desde hace + a time period expresses an action continuing up to now — the same idea as Llevo seis años viviendo en este pueblo, built with llevar + gerund instead.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Which tense does the verb take with desde hace, present or past?","Where does desde hace go in the sentence?","Verb in the present, then the place, then desde hace + time."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'The best thing about this school is the teachers, but the hardest thing is the exams.'","a":"Lo mejor de este colegio son los profesores, pero lo más difícil son los exámenes.","sol":"Lo mejor de este colegio son los profesores, pero lo más difícil son los exámenes. lo + adjective (lo mejor, lo más difícil) turns the adjective into an abstract noun; son agrees with the plural noun that follows (profesores, exámenes), not with lo.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure turns an adjective into 'the [adjective] thing'?","Does the verb that follows agree with that structure itself, or with the noun that comes after it?","Two clauses joined by pero, each starting with lo + adjective."],"codes":["Adjectival phrases"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have neither the time nor the money to go on holiday this year.'","a":"No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para irme de vacaciones este año.","sol":"No tengo ni tiempo ni dinero para irme de vacaciones este año. no … ni … ni … gives 'neither … nor', and the article is normally dropped after ni; para + infinitive expresses purpose.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which structure gives 'neither … nor', and does it need no as well before the verb?","What links 'the money' to 'to go on holiday' — which small word means 'to/in order to'?","No + tener, ni X ni Y, then para + infinitive."],"codes":["Negation"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'This bridge was designed by a famous architect, and it has just been repaired.'","a":"Este puente fue diseñado por un arquitecto famoso, y acaba de ser reparado.","sol":"Este puente fue diseñado por un arquitecto famoso, y acaba de ser reparado. fue diseñado por names who did the action, so it uses ser + past participle + por, not the se-passive; acaba de ser reparado combines acabar de + infinitive with a second passive.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Since 'by a famous architect' is stated, which passive structure fits — se, or ser + participle + por?","'has just been repaired' needs a structure for 'have just done' something, followed by a second passive.","Write the complete sentence with both passive structures."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Marta runs fastest of all the runners, but she says it isn't worth competing if you don't enjoy it.'","a":"Marta corre más rápido que todos los corredores, pero dice que no vale la pena competir si no lo disfrutas.","sol":"Marta corre más rápido que todos los corredores, pero dice que no vale la pena competir si no lo disfrutas. 'Runs fastest of all the runners' is expressed by comparing against the whole group with que todos los corredores (que nadie would also be accepted); no vale la pena + infinitive is the impersonal 'it isn't worth'.","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["How do you compare Marta against the whole group of runners using the ordinary comparative?","Which impersonal phrase means 'it isn't worth'?","Write the complete sentence with both structures."],"codes":["Adverbial phrases","Impersonal verbs"]},{"q":"Write a paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish about how your habits or your town have changed, using at least one Higher tier structure from each of these three ideas: Negation, Inflectional morphology (the subjunctive or a spelling-change verb), and Multi-verb expressions.","a":"A paragraph of about 90 words in Spanish, correctly using at least one structure from each of the three named ideas, describing genuine change over time.","sol":"Antes comía mucha comida basura, pero ya no como tanto porque quiero cuidarme mejor. El año pasado empecé a hacer ejercicio, y llevo tres meses yendo al gimnasio dos veces por semana. También ya no bebo tantos refrescos, y sigo intentando comer más verduras cada semana. Cuando tenga más tiempo el próximo verano, iré al gimnasio todos los días, porque creo que vale la pena. Mi pueblo también ha cambiado: se construyeron varios parques nuevos, y espero que mi pueblo tenga más parques en el futuro. (86 words: ya no appears twice for negation; empecé is a credit-bearing preterite spelling change (z→c), and cuando tenga / espero que ... tenga are genuine present subjunctive of tener, both for inflectional morphology; llevo... yendo and sigo intentando are multi-verb expressions.)","m":15,"d":3,"hints":["Choose one clear example of each of the three required ideas before you start writing.","Describe genuine change: something you used to do, something you do now, and how a place has changed.","Write the paragraph, then check it against your three chosen structures and count the words."],"codes":["Negation","Inflectional morphology","Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Ya no vivo en Madrid; acabo de mudarme a Sevilla, aunque sigo trabajando para la misma empresa.'","a":"I no longer live in Madrid; I have just moved to Seville, although I keep on working for the same company.","sol":"Ya no vivo en Madrid; acabo de mudarme a Sevilla, aunque sigo trabajando para la misma empresa — I no longer live in Madrid; I have just moved to Seville, although I keep on working for the same company. ya no = no longer; acabo de + infinitive = have just; sigo + gerund = keep on.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["ya no changes the meaning of the first verb — how?","acabar de and seguir + gerund each carry a time meaning.","Three linked clauses in one English sentence."],"codes":["Negation","Multi-verb expressions"]},{"q":"Lee: 'El puente fue construido por mi abuelo, y cuando tenga dinero lo voy a reparar, porque vale la pena conservarlo.' Answer in English: who built the bridge, and when does the writer plan to repair it?","a":"The writer's grandfather built it; the writer will repair it when they have money.","sol":"fue construido por mi abuelo — it was built by my grandfather (passive with ser + participle + por); cuando tenga dinero lo voy a reparar — when I have money I'm going to repair it (cuando + present subjunctive for a future event); vale la pena conservarlo — it's worth preserving it.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the passive phrase naming who did the building.","Find the cuando clause giving the condition for repairing it.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Multi-verb expressions","Inflectional morphology","Impersonal verbs"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'not … either'.","a":"tampoco"},{"q":"Translate: 'lo mejor de la fiesta'.","a":"the best thing about the party"},{"q":"How is the se-passive formed, instead of ser + past participle?","a":"se + a third-person verb (se construyó)."},{"q":"What does acabar de + infinitive mean?","a":"To have just done something."}]},"cards":[{"front":"ya no","back":"no longer. Ya no vivo aquí. (I no longer live here.)","code":"Negation","open":false},{"front":"(no) tampoco","back":"not ... either, neither. A mí tampoco me gusta. (I don't like it either.)","code":"Negation","open":false},{"front":"cuando + subjuntivo (futuro)","back":"when [not yet true] + subjunctive. Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré. (When I have time, I'll call you.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"saqué, llegué, cojo","back":"credit-bearing spelling changes (sacar c→qu, llegar g→gu, coger present g→j). Ayer saqué fotos del castillo. (Yesterday I took photos of the castle.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"pidió, durmió","back":"credit-bearing preterite stem changes in -ir verbs (pedir e→i, dormir o→u). El camarero le pidió el pasaporte. (The waiter asked him for his passport.)","code":"Inflectional morphology","open":false},{"front":"acabar de + infinitivo","back":"to have just done something. Acabo de llegar. (I have just arrived.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"ser + participio + por","back":"passive, naming who did it. Fue construido por mi abuelo. (It was built by my grandfather.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"se + verbo (pasiva)","back":"passive, without naming who. Se construyó en 1950. (It was built in 1950.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"llevar + tiempo + gerundio","back":"to have been doing something for [time]. Llevo dos años estudiando español. (I've been studying Spanish for two years.)","code":"Multi-verb expressions","open":false},{"front":"vale la pena + infinitivo","back":"it's worth doing. No vale la pena discutir. (It's not worth arguing.)","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"hace falta / falta + infinitivo o sustantivo","back":"it's necessary to / ... is needed. Hace falta estudiar más. (It's necessary to study more.)","code":"Impersonal verbs","open":false},{"front":"lo + adjetivo","back":"the [adjective] thing. Lo bueno es que tenemos tiempo. (The good thing is we have time.)","code":"Adjectival phrases","open":false},{"front":"más rápido / peor + que nadie","back":"faster / worse than anyone — a superlative meaning. Corre más rápido que nadie. (He runs faster than anyone.)","code":"Adverbial phrases","open":false},{"front":"antes de / después de + infinitivo","back":"before / after doing something. Antes de salir, cierro la puerta. (Before leaving, I lock the door.)","code":"Prepositions","open":false},{"front":"¿Con quién...? / ¿De qué...?","back":"With whom...? / About what...? — preposition stays with the question word. ¿Con quién hablas? (Who are you talking with?)","code":"Prepositions","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: negación y verbos impersonales","items":["ya no - no longer","tampoco - not... either, neither","ni... ni... - neither... nor","parece que - it seems that","basta con - it's enough to","hace falta / falta - it's necessary / ... is needed","vale la pena - it's worth it"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: estructuras de tiempo, comparativos y palabras clave","items":["acabar de + infinitivo - to have just done","seguir + gerundio - to keep on doing","llevar + tiempo + gerundio - to have been doing for [time]","desde hace + tiempo - for [time], continuing to now","antes de + infinitivo - before doing","después de + infinitivo - after doing","el mejor / el peor - the best / the worst","el arquitecto - the architect","el puente - the bridge"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: subjuntivo, pasiva y superlativos","items":["subjuntivo presente (hacer, ser, ir, venir, tener) tras cuando (futuro), deseo/mandato/emoción, y para que. Example: Cuando tenga tiempo, te llamaré. (When I have time, I'll call you.)","imperativo afirmativo (tú, vosotros), irregular en algunos verbos. Example: ¡Ven aquí y ten cuidado! (Come here and be careful!)","pasiva con ser + participio + por (con agente) o se + verbo (sin agente). Example: Este puente fue construido por mi abuelo. (This bridge was built by my grandfather.)","posesivos después del verbo (complemento). Example: Este libro es mío, pero esos boletos son nuestros. (This book is mine, but those tickets are ours.)","superlativo: el/la/los/las + más/menos + adjetivo + de. Comparativo: más/menos + adjetivo + que. Example: Es la estudiante más trabajadora de la clase. (She is the hardest-working student in the class.)","superlativo adverbial: más/menos (o mejor/peor) + adverbio + que nadie/que todos. Example: Corre más rápido que nadie. (He runs faster than anyone — the fastest.)","cambios ortográficos, con crédito a partir de Higher tier: sacar → saqué; llegar → llegué; coger → cojo. Example: Ayer saqué fotos y llegué tarde a casa. (Yesterday I took photos and got home late.)","cambios en la raíz del pretérito (-ir): pedir → pidió/pidieron; dormir → durmió/durmieron. Example: El niño pidió agua y luego durmió toda la noche. (The child asked for water and then slept all night.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.541Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key checks out and the translations are sound Spanish, but the model paragraph awards itself a z→c spelling change (empecé) that the lesson and grammar sheet never teach, and the adverbial-superlative example uses más alone for 'the hardest', so fix those two before publishing."}}},"3.2.3":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"3.2.3","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"This room covers the sound-spelling correspondences Spanish uses differently from English, and the Higher tier rules for where stress falls and when an accent is written. Reading an unfamiliar word aloud accurately, and spelling a word correctly from how it sounds, both depend on knowing these patterns — a misread j, a missed accent, or an rr written as a single r changes the word even when its meaning is one you already know.","idea":[{"code":"3.2.3","h":"How Spanish letters and letter groups are pronounced, where this differs from English","t":"Vowels are always short and pure, never gliding as English vowels do: a as in father (alto, antes), o as in more but shorter (dos, cinco), e as in bed (español, medio), i as in machine (idea, iglesia), u as in rude (universidad, instituto). ll sounds like the y in yes (llamar, calle); ch is always as in cheese, never split into a hard c plus h (leche, ocho). c is hard, as in cat, before a, o, u (casa, coche, escuchar) but soft, close to English s or th, before e or i (cerca, concierto); when cu is immediately followed by another vowel in the same syllable, the whole cluster sounds like 'kw' (cuando, cuatro); que and qui keep the hard sound with a silent u (porque, tranquilo). z is always the same soft sound as c before e or i (zona, manzana). g is hard, as in go, before a, o, u (ganar, tengo, preguntar) but a harsh, throaty sound with no English equivalent before e or i (gente, gimnasio); gue and gui keep the hard g sound with a silent u before e or i (hamburguesa, guitarra). j is always that same harsh, throaty sound as g before e/i (ojo, naranja). ñ is a single sound like the ny in canyon, never n plus y (mañana, cumpleaños). v is pronounced the same as b, not as an English v (verde, verdad). Between vowels, or at the end of a word, a single r is a quick tap of the tongue (pero, hablar); rr, r at the very start of a word, and a single r straight after n, l or s all count as the same rolled, trilled sound (cerrar, rojo, Enrique, alrededor) — a single r after n, l or s is not a tap, it is treated exactly like rr. h is always silent (hospital, hoy) — it is never pronounced, whatever letters follow it."},{"code":"3.2.3.1","h":"Higher tier: where the stress falls, and when to write an accent","t":"Higher tier only. Reading aloud: if a word carries a written accent, stress that vowel, wherever it falls (rápido, está, médico). If there is no written accent, a word ending in a vowel, n or s is stressed on its second-to-last (penultimate) syllable (ca-SA, ha-BLAN, LI-bros); a word ending in any other consonant is stressed on its last (final) syllable (ha-BLAR, ciu-DAD, re-LOJ). Transcribing (writing down what you hear): work out which syllable is stressed, then apply the same rule in reverse. If the stress falls where the rule already predicts — penultimate for a word ending in a vowel/n/s, final for a word ending in another consonant — no accent is written. If the stress falls somewhere the rule does not predict, an accent is written on the stressed vowel: on a final syllable ending in a vowel, n or s (ha-BLÓ, in-GLÉS, es-TÁN); or on a penultimate syllable of a word ending in a consonant other than n or s (LÁ-piz, FÁ-cil). A word stressed on its third-to-last (antepenultimate) syllable always takes an accent, whatever it ends in (RÁ-pi-do, TE-lé-fo-no, MÚ-si-ca)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a sentence aloud: hard and soft c, and the throaty g","setup":"You must read this sentence aloud: \"El cocinero prepara la comida con mucha paciencia y gente amable.\" State the sound of each plain c in cocinero, comida, con and paciencia, say whether mucha contains a plain c, and give the sound of g in gente.","steps":["cocinero: the first c is before o (hard, as in cat) and the second is before i (soft, close to English s).","comida and con: in both, the c is before o, so it is hard, the same as the first c in cocinero.","paciencia: both of its c's come before i (pa-cien-cia), so both are soft; mucha has no plain c — its ch is the single digraph sound, as in cheese.","gente has g before e, which is the harsh, throaty sound, not the hard g of gato."],"cues":["What vowel follows each of the two c's in cocinero?","What vowel follows the c in comida and in con?","What vowel follows each c in paciencia, and does mucha contain a plain c?","What vowel follows the g in gente?"]},{"title":"Distinguishing pero and perro by ear, for a dictation task","setup":"In your dictation you hear: \"El [?] come, [?] no bebe agua\", where the two unclear words sound alike apart from their r. Explain how you tell perro (dog) and pero (but) apart, by their sound and by their job in the sentence, and write the sentence correctly spelled.","steps":["perro and pero differ by only one letter, but that letter changes the sound completely: perro has the long rolled/trilled rr, pero has the quick single tap — so listen first for the length of the r.","The first word, right after El, is the subject that comes (eats) — a noun, not a joining word, so it must be perro (dog), with the rolled rr sound.","The second word joins the two halves of the sentence with a contrast ('eats, but doesn't drink') — a joining word, not a noun, so it must be pero (but), with the quick tapped r.","Full sentence, correctly spelled: El perro come, pero no bebe agua."],"cues":["What is the one spelling difference between perro and pero, and how does it sound?","Is the first word a noun (the thing doing the eating) or a joining word?","Is the second word a noun or a joining word, given it links two contrasting actions?","Write out the whole sentence with both words spelled correctly."]},{"title":"Deciding whether a word needs a written accent, reading aloud","setup":"You hear these two words stressed as follows when a Spanish speaker says them: ha-BLO (stress on the last syllable) and ME-di-co (stress on the third-to-last syllable). Decide whether each needs a written accent, and where it goes.","steps":["hablo ends in a vowel (o) and is stressed on its final syllable — the rule predicts penultimate stress for a word ending in a vowel, so this stress is not where the rule predicts.","Because the actual stress does not match what the ending predicts, this word needs a written accent on the stressed vowel: habló.","medico is stressed on its third-to-last (antepenultimate) syllable — the specification's rule says every word stressed there always takes a written accent, regardless of what letter it ends in.","So this word needs an accent on its third-to-last syllable: médico."],"cues":["What letter does hablo end in, and which syllable does the rule predict for that ending?","Does the actual stress on hablo match that prediction — so does it need an accent?","Which syllable is medico stressed on, and what does the rule say about that position?","So does medico need an accent, and does its ending matter for this one?"]},{"title":"Reading aloud: silent h and the ñ sound","setup":"You must read this sentence aloud: \"El niño español no habla mucho hoy porque está en el hospital.\" Identify every silent h and explain how ñ is pronounced.","steps":["Scan the sentence for every letter h: habla, hoy, hospital each begin with h.","In Spanish, h is never pronounced at all, whatever letter follows it — so habla starts with the sound of a, hoy with the sound of oy, and hospital with the sound of ospital.","ñ is one single sound, like the ny in the English word canyon — niño is pronounced NEE-nyo as one smooth sound, not nee-no (dropping the y sound).","Pulling both parts together: the silent h's are in habla, hoy and hospital (read as abla, oy, ospital), and ñ — in niño and again in español — is the single ny sound."],"cues":["Which three words in the sentence begin with the letter h?","How is that letter h pronounced in each of those three words?","What single English sound is ñ closest to?","Bring both parts together: which words have a silent h, and how is ñ said?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which letter or letters represent the throaty, scraped sound (like the ch in Scottish loch) in jamón, and which two letter combinations represent the same sound before e or i?","a":"j; and g before e or i (as in gente, gigante)."},{"q":"A word ends in a consonant other than n or s and has no written accent. Which syllable is stressed?","a":"The final (last) syllable."},{"q":"How is v pronounced in Spanish?","a":"The same as b, not as an English v."},{"q":"What is the difference in sound between a single r between vowels and rr?","a":"A single r between vowels is one quick tap of the tongue; rr — and a single r after n, l or s — is a longer rolled or trilled sound, treated the same as rr."}]},"room":{"facts":["h is always silent in Spanish, whatever letters follow it (hospital, hoy).","c and g are hard before a, o, u but change before e or i: c becomes soft (like English s); g becomes a throaty, scraped sound, the same as j.","que, qui, gue, gui keep the hard sound of c and g with a silent u before e or i.","A single r between vowels, or at the end of a word, is a quick tap; rr, r at the start of a word, and a single r straight after n, l or s are all the same longer rolled sound.","Accent rule (Higher tier): no accent needed when stress falls on the penultimate syllable of a word ending in a vowel/n/s, or the final syllable of a word ending in any other consonant; an accent is written whenever the actual stress falls somewhere else, and always for antepenultimate stress."],"questions":[{"q":"In the word cocina, is the first c hard (as in cat) or soft (like an English s)?","a":"Hard","sol":"Hard, as in cat: the first c in cocina is followed by o, and c is hard before a, o and u (the second c, before i, is soft).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Which vowel follows the first c in this word?","Back vowels (a, o, u) and front vowels (e, i) give c two different sounds.","One word naming the sound type."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Is the g in guitarra pronounced as a hard g (as in go) or as the throaty, scraped sound (as in j)?","a":"Hard g","sol":"Hard g, as in go: gui keeps the hard sound of g with a silent u before i, the same pattern as gue.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["gui and gue are a special pair — what do they do to the u?","Compare with gente, which has no u after the g.","A short phrase naming the sound type."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Give the English sound closest to Spanish ll, as in llamar.","a":"The y in yes","sol":"The y in yes: ll (and usually y) represents that sound in Spanish, not an English l sound.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is not the same sound as an English double l.","Think of a common English word starting with the sound you want.","Name the sound with one short English example."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Which letter in reloj makes the throaty, scraped sound (the same sound as g before e or i)?","a":"j","sol":"The j at the end of reloj makes the harsh, throaty sound, pronounced the same way as g before e or i; reloj contains no silent letter at all.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the letters in reloj — which one shares its sound with g before e/i?","Say the word aloud slowly and listen for the scraped sound.","Name just the one letter."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Is the r in Enrique pronounced as a quick tap or as a rolled sound, and why?","a":"Rolled — because a single r straight after n counts as the same sound as rr.","sol":"The r in Enrique comes straight after the consonant n, and a single r after n, l or s is treated as the same rolled, trilled sound as rr, not the quick tap used between vowels.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the position of the r in the word, not just the letter itself.","Which positions make a single r sound like rr?","Name the sound type, then the reason in one clause."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"This sentence needs two written accents: 'El medico hablo con su hermana.' Identify both words and add the accents.","a":"médico, habló","sol":"El médico habló con su hermana. médico is stressed on its third-to-last syllable, which always takes an accent; habló is stressed on its final syllable but ends in a vowel, where the rule predicts penultimate stress, so the actual final-syllable stress must be marked with an accent.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate words in this sentence need an accent — find both before writing either.","For each one, work out which syllable is actually stressed, and compare that with what the ending predicts.","Rewrite the two words with their accents; the rest of the sentence is unchanged."],"codes":["3.2.3.1"]},{"q":"In 'La luz del sol es muy fuerte', what sound does the z in luz make?","a":"The same soft sound as c before e or i.","sol":"z always makes the same soft sound as c before e or i, whichever vowel follows or precedes it; in luz it comes at the end of the word, but the sound is unchanged.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the sound of z with one of the two sounds c can make.","This sound does not change depending on position in the word.","Name the sound z always makes."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: \"Mi tío tiene un perro pequeño. Cada mañana el perro corre por el jardín y luego bebe agua. Pero cuando llueve, se queda en casa.\" (a) What does the dog do every morning? (b) What happens when it rains?","a":"(a) It runs around the garden and then drinks water. (b) It stays at home (when it rains).","sol":"(a) Cada mañana el perro corre por el jardín y luego bebe agua — every morning the dog runs around the garden and then drinks water. (b) Pero cuando llueve, se queda en casa — but when it rains, it stays at home. Reading perro correctly (rolled rr, meaning dog) rather than pero (but) is essential to following who is doing what in this text.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the sentence describing the morning routine, and pick out the two actions in it.","The second question starts after the word pero (but) — read on from there.","Two answers: the two morning actions, then what happens in the rain."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, taking care over spelling and accents: 'The young boy speaks a little Spanish and lives in a village.'","a":"El niño habla un poco de español y vive en un pueblo.","sol":"El niño habla un poco de español y vive en un pueblo. niño and español both need ñ, not plain n; habla has no accent (stressed on the penultimate syllable of a word ending in a vowel, exactly where the rule predicts).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which sounds in this sentence does Spanish spell with a letter English does not have?","Check each verb's stress against the ending rule before deciding on accents.","One complete Spanish sentence: subject, verb, object, y, verb, place."],"codes":["3.2.3","3.2.3.1"]},{"q":"Give the English for this exchange, paying attention to hard and soft c and g: '¿Dónde está la cocina? Está cerca del jardín grande.'","a":"'Where is the kitchen?' 'It's near the big garden.'","sol":"¿Dónde está la cocina? — Where is the kitchen? Está cerca del jardín grande — It's near the big garden.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["cocina and cerca each contain more than one c — check each one's following vowel separately.","jardín starts with the throaty, scraped sound, the same as g before e/i.","Give the English translation of both sentences."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Explain, for someone reading this word aloud for the first time, exactly how h behaves in hospital.","a":"It is silent — never pronounced, wherever it falls in the word.","sol":"h is always silent in Spanish, in every position and whatever letters surround it, so hospital is pronounced as if it began with the sound o, not h.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Is this letter ever pronounced anywhere in Spanish?","Say what sound the word actually starts with once you read past this letter.","One short explanation."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"Write a short paragraph in Spanish (about 40 words) describing your town, using at least one example of ll, one of rr, one of j, and at least two words containing silent h.","a":"e.g. Vivo en un pueblo pequeño con una calle principal y muchas casas amarillas. Hay un hospital, un hotel y un jardín bonito con un perro que corre todos los días. Hoy hace calor y los niños juegan cerca del ayuntamiento.","sol":"Vivo en un pueblo pequeño con una calle principal y muchas casas amarillas. Hay un hospital, un hotel y un jardín bonito con un perro que corre todos los días. Hoy hace calor y los niños juegan cerca del ayuntamiento. (40 words: calle/amarillas give ll; perro/corre give rr; jardín/juegan give j; hay/hospital/hotel/hoy give silent h.)","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["List the four sound types you must include before you start writing, and choose one word for each.","Silent h needs at least two separate words, not the same word twice.","Write the paragraph, then check it against your list word by word."],"codes":["3.2.3"]},{"q":"This dictation sentence was mis-transcribed as 'El nino canto una cancion bonita.' Correct every missing accent or tilde, and explain each one.","a":"El niño cantó una canción bonita — niño needs ñ (a different letter); cantó and canción are stressed on a final syllable ending in a vowel/n, so both need an accent.","sol":"niño needs ñ (a different letter from n, not an accent) for the ny sound. cantó is stressed on its final syllable but ends in a vowel, where the rule predicts penultimate stress, so it needs an accent. canción is stressed on its final syllable and ends in n, where the rule also predicts penultimate stress, so the final-syllable stress needs an accent too.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Go through the sentence word by word, checking each one against the sound-spelling rules.","For any word whose stress does not fall where the ending predicts, check what mark is missing.","Rewrite the full sentence with every correction in place."],"codes":["3.2.3","3.2.3.1"]},{"q":"Explain why cuando is pronounced with a 'kw' sound but escuchar is not, giving the rule.","a":"In cuando, cu is followed directly by another vowel (a) in the same syllable, so the cluster cu + vowel is pronounced 'kw'; in escuchar, cu is followed by a consonant (ch), so the u is an ordinary vowel and c is a plain hard k.","sol":"cuando: cu + a within one syllable — the u works as a glide, so the cluster sounds like the 'qu' of English 'quick'. escuchar: cu is followed by the consonant ch, so u is a full vowel ('oo') and the c before it is a plain hard k — es-cu-char. The rule: cu directly before another vowel = 'kw'; cu before a consonant = k + oo.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Look at what letter comes straight after cu in each word.","Say each word slowly — in which one does the u keep its full \"oo\" sound?","Give the sound of cu in each word, then the rule in one sentence."],"codes":["3.2.3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How is v pronounced in Spanish?","a":"The same as b."},{"q":"Does luz end with a hard or soft consonant sound, and what letter makes it?","a":"Soft, like English s — made by the letter z."},{"q":"Give one word where c is hard and one where c is soft.","a":"Hard: casa, cosa, cuna (before a/o/u). Soft: cero, cine (before e/i)."},{"q":"Which syllable is stressed in a word with no written accent that ends in n?","a":"The penultimate (second-to-last) syllable."}]},"cards":[{"front":"alto","back":"tall, high","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"llamar","back":"to call, to phone","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"cocina","back":"kitchen","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"cerca","back":"near, close","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"porque","back":"because","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"zona","back":"zone, area","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"tengo","back":"I have","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"gente","back":"people","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"guitarra","back":"guitar","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"naranja","back":"orange","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"mañana","back":"tomorrow, morning","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"hospital","back":"hospital","code":"3.2.3","open":false},{"front":"médico","back":"doctor","code":"3.2.3.1","open":false},{"front":"habló","back":"he/she spoke","code":"3.2.3.1","open":false},{"front":"fácil","back":"easy","code":"3.2.3.1","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: pares mínimos y ejemplos de pronunciación","items":["pero - but (single r, tapped)","perro - dog (rr, rolled)","casa - house (hard c)","cero - zero (soft c)","gato - cat (hard g)","gente - people (throaty g)","guitarra - guitar (hard g, silent u)","hospital - hospital (silent h)","hoy - today (silent h)","niño / niña - boy / girl (ñ)","español - Spanish (ñ)","luz - light (z, soft)","reloj - clock, watch (j, throaty)"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario: palabras con acento escrito","items":["médico - doctor (antepenultimate stress)","teléfono - telephone (antepenultimate stress)","rápido - fast, quick (antepenultimate stress)","habló - he/she spoke (final syllable, ends in vowel)","está - he/she/it is (final syllable, ends in vowel)","inglés - English (final syllable, ends in s)","canción - song (final syllable, ends in n)","fácil - easy (penultimate syllable, ends in l)","lápiz - pencil (penultimate syllable, ends in z)","difícil - difficult (penultimate syllable, ends in l)"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: las reglas de acentuación","items":["Sin acento escrito, penúltima sílaba si la palabra termina en vocal, n o s. Example: Mis hermanos hablan español. (My brothers speak Spanish.)","Sin acento escrito, última sílaba si la palabra termina en otra consonante. Example: Voy a hablar con el profesor. (I'm going to speak with the teacher.)","Con acento escrito cuando el golpe de voz no coincide con la regla, por ejemplo en la última sílaba de una palabra que termina en vocal. Example: Ayer mi hermano habló con el médico. (Yesterday my brother spoke with the doctor.)","La sílaba antepenúltima siempre lleva acento escrito. Example: Mi médico tiene un teléfono nuevo. (My doctor has a new phone.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.542Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the specification's SSC list and Higher-tier stress rules are covered faithfully; fix the 'every letter h' step that overlooks the ch in mucho, the instruction-shaped cue in the pero/perro example, and the two hints (Q5 on Enrique, Q14 on cuando) that leak the discriminating point of their answers."}}},"Foundation tier vocabulary":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"Foundation tier vocabulary","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"Appendix 2 is the word list every Foundation tier reading, listening, writing and speaking task is built from, and two columns printed beside each headword tell you exactly what you are responsible for: the Tier column shows which items you need, and the Selection-principle column shows how each one was chosen. Knowing how to read the columns tells you exactly which words you are responsible for, and knowing the words themselves earns marks everywhere else — so this room covers both the coding system and real use of a representative sample of the words.","idea":[{"code":"Tier F","h":"The Tier column: F and F/H","t":"Headwords in Appendix 2 carry the code F to mean they belong to the Foundation tier list. A smaller set — mainly the 30 multi-word phrases (such as me gustaría, tengo que, hace buen tiempo) and 20 geographical or cultural items (such as España) — carries the code F/H instead, meaning that item is required at both Foundation and Higher tier: it must be known productively (speaking and writing) and receptively (listening and reading) at Foundation tier, and it carries forward unchanged into the Higher tier list rather than being replaced by something else. The specification does not state that a plain F headword is excluded from Higher tier study — F simply marks it as a Foundation tier item; F/H is the code that explicitly flags an item as shared between the two tiers."},{"code":"Selection principle","h":"The Selection-principle column: R, O, O>, C","t":"Each headword also carries a Selection-principle code, explained in the key printed under the vocabulary table. R (Required) marks a word that is part of the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words. O (Optional) marks a word drawn from within the top 2000 most frequent words in Spanish that is not on the Annex E list. O> (Optional) marks a word drawn from outside the top 2000 most frequent words — that is the whole of the definition given, so as far as the key is concerned a word counts as O> once it falls outside that frequency band, however specialised it is. C marks the culturally or geographically specific items — festival names, place names and similar — which are listed regardless of how frequent they are, because they are included for what they refer to rather than for their frequency."}],"examples":[{"title":"Identifying an F/H item and using it in a sentence","setup":"The appendix lists 'me gustaría' with the code F/H. Explain what this code means for a Foundation tier candidate, and write one Spanish sentence using me gustaría to say 'I would like to visit Spain'.","steps":["F/H means this phrase is required at both Foundation and Higher tier, so a Foundation candidate must already know it productively and receptively, and it carries forward unchanged into Higher tier study.","me gustaría is followed directly by an infinitive to say what you would like to do: me gustaría visitar.","Add the place: visitar España.","Put together: F/H means it is required at both tiers; the sentence is Me gustaría visitar España."],"cues":["What does the code F/H tell you about who must know this phrase?","What form of the verb follows me gustaría?","Which country are you saying you would like to visit?","Give both parts of the answer: the meaning of the code, and the full sentence."]},{"title":"Reading comprehension: a reason clause with porque","setup":"Translate into English: 'Mis padres van a la playa porque hace buen tiempo.'","steps":["Mis padres van a la playa translates as 'My parents go/are going to the beach'.","porque introduces the reason and translates simply as 'because'.","hace buen tiempo is a weather phrase meaning 'the weather is good' or 'it's nice weather'.","Full English answer: My parents are going to the beach because the weather is good."],"cues":["Who is doing the action, and where are they going?","What English word does porque translate to?","What does hace buen tiempo describe?","Write the complete English sentence."]},{"title":"Translating into Spanish: a hobby with jugar al","setup":"Translate into Spanish: 'I play chess every Sunday.'","steps":["'I play' uses jugar, which changes its stem to jueg- in the yo form: juego.","'chess' follows jugar al, since ajedrez is masculine: juego al ajedrez.","'every Sunday' is todos los domingos, added at the end.","Put together: Juego al ajedrez todos los domingos."],"cues":["What is the yo form of jugar, with its stem change?","Which preposition and article combine before ajedrez?","How do you say 'every Sunday'?","Write the complete Spanish sentence."]},{"title":"Using a C-coded cultural item in a sentence","setup":"The appendix lists 'España' with the code C. Explain what the C code means here, and write one Spanish sentence saying 'Spain is a beautiful country.'","steps":["C marks España as a geographically specific item, listed because of what it refers to rather than because of how frequent the word is.","'Spain is' translates as España es.","'a beautiful country' is un país precioso, with país masculine so the article and adjective agree.","Put together: C means it is a geographical item listed regardless of frequency; the sentence is España es un país precioso."],"cues":["What kind of item does the code C mark — is it about frequency, or about subject matter?","How do you say 'Spain is' in Spanish?","What gender is país, and how does that affect the article and adjective?","Give both parts of the answer: the meaning of the code, and the full sentence."]}],"check":[{"q":"What does the Tier code F/H mean for a headword in Appendix 2?","a":"It is required at both Foundation and Higher tier, known productively and receptively, and carries forward unchanged into Higher tier."},{"q":"What is the difference between R and O in the Selection-principle column?","a":"R marks a word on the DfE's Annex E grammar-linked list; O marks a word within the top 2000 most frequent words that is not on that list."},{"q":"Does the specification set any further test for an O>-coded word beyond being outside the top 2000 most frequent words?","a":"No — any word chosen for the topic that falls outside the top 2000 most frequent words counts as O>."},{"q":"Translate: 'hace buen tiempo'.","a":"The weather is good / it's nice weather."}]},"room":{"facts":["Tier column: F = Foundation tier; F/H = required at both Foundation and Higher tier, carried forward unchanged.","Selection-principle column: R = on the DfE's Annex E grammar-linked list; O = optional, within the top 2000 most frequent words; O> = optional, outside the top 2000 most frequent words; C = cultural/geographical item, listed regardless of frequency.","me gustaría + infinitive = I would like to; tengo que + infinitive = I have to; hace buen/mal tiempo = the weather is good/bad.","jugar changes its stem to jueg- except in the nosotros/vosotros forms, and is followed by al before a masculine activity: jugar al fútbol, jugar al ajedrez.","porque (because, mid-sentence) introduces a reason; the same idea to open a sentence is como."],"questions":[{"q":"What does the Tier code F mean for a headword in Appendix 2?","a":"It is a Foundation tier headword.","sol":"F marks a word as belonging to the Foundation tier list — the level of vocabulary every Foundation candidate is expected to know.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is the more common of the two Tier codes.","Which single tier does this code name on its own?","One short phrase."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Which Selection-principle code marks a word chosen for its cultural or geographical content rather than for how frequent it is?","a":"C","sol":"C marks the culturally or geographically specific items — such as festival and place names — included regardless of frequency, unlike R, O and O>, which are all frequency-related.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This code stands apart from the three frequency-based codes.","Think of a festival name or place name in the appendix.","One letter."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Me gustaría ir a la playa este fin de semana.'","a":"I would like to go to the beach this weekend.","sol":"I would like to go to the beach this weekend. me gustaría + infinitive (ir) gives 'I would like to go'; este fin de semana is 'this weekend'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["me gustaría is followed by an infinitive — what does that combination mean?","Where does the sentence say this person would like to go?","Give the complete English sentence, including when."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'I have to study on Saturdays.'","a":"Tengo que estudiar los sábados.","sol":"Tengo que estudiar los sábados. tengo que + infinitive gives 'I have to'; 'on Saturdays' (a repeated day) is los sábados, no separate word for 'on'.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which fixed phrase means 'I have to'?","What follows the 'have to' phrase — an infinitive or a conjugated verb?","One complete sentence."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An appendix entry is marked 'O'. Explain what this tells you, and give one difference between O and O>.","a":"O means the word is optional and falls within the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words; O> is also optional but falls outside the top 2000.","sol":"O marks an optional word (not on the Annex E required list) that is still within the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words; the difference from O> is purely this frequency band — O> is optional but rarer, falling outside the top 2000.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Is an O-coded word required, or optional?","Compare O directly with O> — what single thing changes between them?","Give two things: what O means, and the one difference from O>."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Read this text and answer in English: 'Vivo en un pueblo pequeño cerca de la costa. Me gusta porque hay una playa bonita y el ambiente es tranquilo.' What does the writer like about where they live, and why?","a":"They like their village: it has a pretty beach and a calm/peaceful atmosphere.","sol":"The writer likes their village (Me gusta) because there is a pretty beach (hay una playa bonita) and the atmosphere is calm/peaceful (el ambiente es tranquilo) — three points: what they like, and the two reasons joined after porque.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the reason word porque in the second sentence.","What comes after porque?","Answer in English, saying what they like and why."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'My parents watch television every evening, but I prefer to play football.'","a":"Mis padres ven la televisión todas las noches, pero yo prefiero jugar al fútbol.","sol":"Mis padres ven la televisión todas las noches, pero yo prefiero jugar al fútbol. ver is irregular in the ellos form (ven); prefiero is a stem-changing verb (e→ie) followed by the infinitive jugar, which itself takes al before fútbol. 'Every evening' may be todas las tardes or todas las noches; both are accepted.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["This verb is irregular for 'they' — check its form before writing.","The verb for 'prefer' is followed directly by another verb — in which form?","One sentence with two linked clauses."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An entry is marked with the Selection-principle code R. What does this tell you about how it was chosen, and how does that differ from an O-coded entry?","a":"R means the word is part of the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words; O means the word is within the top 2000 most frequent words but not on that Annex E list.","sol":"R and O rest on different grounds: R marks a word as belonging to the DfE's Annex E list of grammar-linked words, while O marks a word chosen purely for being within the top 2000 most frequent words, without being on that Annex E list.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["R is tied to a specific named list, not just frequency.","Which list is that, and what kind of words does it contain?","Explain both codes and how they differ."],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Todos los años vamos a España para visitar a mis abuelos, y siempre hace mucho calor en verano.'","a":"Every year we go to Spain to visit my grandparents, and it's always very hot in summer.","sol":"Every year we go to Spain to visit my grandparents, and it's always very hot in summer. vamos a España is 'we go to Spain' (ir a + place); para visitar is a separate purpose clause, 'in order to visit'; hace mucho calor is a weather phrase meaning 'it is very hot'.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Who is 'we' going to visit, and where?","What season is named at the end, and what weather phrase goes with it?","Give the complete English sentence."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Write five short sentences in Spanish about your free time, each correctly using one of these phrases: me gustaría, tengo que, hace buen tiempo, jugar al + a sport, and quiero + infinitive.","a":"Five sentences, e.g. Me gustaría aprender a nadar. / Tengo que hacer los deberes. / Cuando hace buen tiempo, voy al parque. / Juego al baloncesto los sábados. / Quiero ver una película.","sol":"Example: Me gustaría aprender a nadar este verano. Tengo que hacer los deberes antes de salir. Cuando hace buen tiempo, voy al parque con mis amigos. Los fines de semana juego al baloncesto. Quiero ver una película esta noche. Each sentence uses one target phrase with correct grammar: me gustaría + infinitive, tengo que + infinitive, hace buen tiempo as a time clause, jugar al + a masculine sport, and quiero + infinitive.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Which of the phrases need an infinitive straight after them?","Which phrase describes the weather, and how can it start a sentence?","Five separate, complete sentences."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"Write a short paragraph of approximately 50 words in Spanish about your last holiday, using at least two Foundation tier phrases from this room (for example fui a, me gustó, hacía buen tiempo, jugué al).","a":"About 50 words in the past tense using at least two of: fui a, me gustó, hacía buen tiempo, jugué al.","sol":"El verano pasado fui a España con mi familia. Hacía muy buen tiempo, así que fuimos a la playa casi todos los días. Me gustó mucho porque el mar estaba muy limpio. También jugué al voleibol con mi hermano y comimos paella en un restaurante cerca del hotel. (48 words: fui a, hacía buen tiempo, me gustó and jugué al all appear.)","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Choose at least two phrases from the list before you start, and plan one sentence around each.","Keep every verb in the past tense, since this describes a holiday that has already happened.","Write the paragraph, then count the words and check it is close to 50."],"codes":["Tier F"]},{"q":"An appendix entry reads: 'la enología — O>'. A student says this word is too specialised to really be O>-coded. Using only the specification's own definition of O>, say whether the student is right.","a":"No — the specification's only test for O> is that the word is optional and falls outside the top 2000 most frequent words; it sets no further limit on how specialised a word may be, so la enología can validly be O> if it meets that one condition.","sol":"The student is not right: the specification defines O> only as 'optional, drawn from outside the top 2000 most frequent words'. It sets no further limit on how specialised a word may be within that band, so a specialised word like la enología (oenology, the study of wine) is a valid O> entry provided it genuinely falls outside the top 2000.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Go back to exactly how the specification defines O> — read it carefully.","Separate what the code technically requires from a judgement about whether the word is a sensible choice to include.","State clearly whether the student's objection is supported by the specification's own wording."],"codes":["Selection principle"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which Tier code marks a headword as belonging to the Foundation tier list?","a":"F"},{"q":"Give the Spanish for 'I have to' followed by an infinitive.","a":"Tengo que"},{"q":"A word is coded O rather than O>. Is it more or less frequent than an O>-coded word?","a":"More frequent — O falls within the top 2000 most frequent words, O> falls outside it."},{"q":"Translate: 'jugar al baloncesto'.","a":"to play basketball"}]},"cards":[{"front":"me gustaría + infinitivo","back":"I would like to. Me gustaría visitar España. (I would like to visit Spain.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"tengo que + infinitivo","back":"I have to. Tengo que estudiar. (I have to study.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"hace buen/mal tiempo","back":"the weather is good/bad. Hace buen tiempo hoy. (The weather is good today.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"jugar al + deporte","back":"to play + sport (stem-changing, jueg-). Juego al ajedrez. (I play chess.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"la playa","back":"the beach. Vamos a la playa. (We're going to the beach.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"el fin de semana","back":"the weekend. Este fin de semana voy al parque. (This weekend I'm going to the park.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"los abuelos","back":"grandparents. Visito a mis abuelos. (I visit my grandparents.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"porque","back":"because. Me gusta porque es divertido. (I like it because it's fun.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"España","back":"Spain. España es un país precioso. (Spain is a beautiful country.)","code":"Selection principle","open":false},{"front":"el fútbol","back":"football. Juego al fútbol los sábados. (I play football on Saturdays.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"mucho / mucha / muchos / muchas","back":"a lot of, much, many (agrees with the noun). Hace mucho calor. (It's very hot.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"quiero + infinitivo","back":"I want to. Quiero aprender español. (I want to learn Spanish.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"voy a + infinitivo","back":"I am going to. Voy a jugar al tenis. (I am going to play tennis.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"prefiero + infinitivo","back":"I prefer to. Prefiero jugar al fútbol. (I prefer to play football.)","code":"Tier F","open":false},{"front":"todos los días","back":"every day. Juego al fútbol todos los días. (I play football every day.)","code":"Tier F","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Foundation tier: frases F/H más usadas","items":["me gustaría + infinitivo - I would like to","tengo que + infinitivo - I have to","hace buen tiempo - the weather is good","hace mal tiempo - the weather is bad","jugar al fútbol - to play football","jugar al ajedrez - to play chess","el fin de semana - the weekend","todos los días - every day","el año pasado - last year"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Foundation tier: lugares y familia (F y C)","items":["la playa - the beach","el pueblo - the village, small town","la costa - the coast","el ambiente - the atmosphere","los abuelos - the grandparents","los padres - the parents","el hermano / la hermana - brother / sister","España - Spain","el mar - the sea","el restaurante - the restaurant"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: estructuras F/H frecuentes","items":["me gustaría + infinitivo. Example: Me gustaría visitar España. (I would like to visit Spain.)","tengo que + infinitivo. Example: Tengo que estudiar los sábados. (I have to study on Saturdays.)","jugar al + deporte, con cambio de raíz (jueg-) menos en nosotros/vosotros. Example: Juego al ajedrez, pero mi hermano juega al fútbol. (I play chess, but my brother plays football.)","hace buen/mal tiempo (expresión del tiempo). Example: Hace buen tiempo hoy, así que vamos a la playa. (The weather is good today, so we're going to the beach.)","el pretérito para acciones pasadas y terminadas. Example: El verano pasado fui a España y jugué al voleibol. (Last summer I went to Spain and played volleyball.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.543Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly (translations, comprehension and the coding questions all match the specification map), so the kit is close to publishable; fix the 'todas las noches' key, re-code or replace the España card, and trim the two inferences added to the specification's definitions of F and O."}}},"Higher tier vocabulary":{"id":"AQA-8692","topic":"Higher tier vocabulary","family":"language","lesson":{"why":"The Higher tier vocabulary appendix is the master list of every word and phrase you are expected to know for Papers 1 to 4 at Higher tier, and two of its columns tell you exactly what you are responsible for: the Tier column shows which items are Higher-only and which you should already know from Foundation, and the Selection-principle column shows how central each item is. Examiners set questions using this list, so misreading a column costs marks on words you may otherwise know perfectly well — and the words themselves (hay que, se necesita, tan…como, and the festivals and places named in the appendix) turn up across listening, reading, writing and speaking.","idea":[{"code":"Tier H","h":"The Tier column: H versus F/H","t":"The Tier column prints H against a headword, phrase or irregular form required only at Higher tier — a Foundation tier candidate is not expected to know it. It prints F/H against multi-word phrases and items required at both tiers: structures such as hay que, tener que, hace + a noun, se puede, me/te/le gustaría, se necesita and tan…como, and cultural items such as plaza de toros, fuegos artificiales, Semana Santa, España, Sudamérica and Bachillerato. Because F/H items are shared, a Higher tier candidate must know the complete Foundation tier list as well as everything marked H — the appendix does not repeat the Foundation list separately, it only adds to it."},{"code":"Selection principle","h":"The Selection-principle column: R, O, O>, C","t":"Each entry also carries a Selection-principle code. R (Required) means the item is on the core list you are expected to know. O (Optional) marks an item that falls within the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words but is not on the required core list. O> marks an item that falls outside the top 2000 most frequent words — rarer vocabulary. C marks the cultural and geographical items printed at the end of the appendix — words such as corrida, Día de Reyes, Día de Muertos, Nochevieja, paella, tapas, disfraz, Las Fallas, la Tomatina, los Sanfermines, flamenco and ayuntamiento — grouped separately from the frequency-based R/O/O> codes because they are included for their cultural content rather than their frequency."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading an appendix entry: hace + noun, its tier and its selection principle","setup":"The appendix lists this entry: 'hace + sustantivo — F/H'. State which tier or tiers must know it, and give one example sentence using it to say when something happened.","steps":["F/H in the Tier column means this phrase is required at both Foundation and Higher tier — a Higher tier candidate is still expected to know it, not just Higher-only items.","hace followed by a noun of time means 'ago': the noun says how far back — dos años, una semana, mucho tiempo.","Answer: both tiers must know it; Hace dos años empecé a estudiar español (Two years ago I started studying Spanish)."],"cues":["What does F/H tell you about which tier(s) must know this phrase?","What does hace mean when it is followed by a noun of time?","How would you put it into a sentence, and what is the full answer?"]},{"title":"Reading comprehension: an impersonal structure with hay que","setup":"Translate into English: 'Hay que reciclar el vidrio y el papel para proteger el medio ambiente.'","steps":["hay que + infinitive is an impersonal structure meaning 'it is necessary to' or, more naturally in English, 'you have to' — it does not name who must do it.","reciclar el vidrio y el papel translates as 'recycle the glass and paper' — the two objects joined by y.","para proteger el medio ambiente translates as 'to protect the environment' — para plus an infinitive gives purpose, 'in order to'.","Full English answer: You have to recycle the glass and paper to protect the environment."],"cues":["What does hay que mean, and does it name a specific person who must act?","What two things does the sentence say must be recycled?","What does para plus an infinitive add to the sentence?","Write the complete English sentence."]},{"title":"Translating into Spanish with gustaría and tan…como","setup":"Translate into Spanish, using the informal singular form of 'you': 'You would like to visit South America because it is as interesting as Spain.'","steps":["'You' is specified as informal singular, so the conditional of gustar takes te: te gustaría.","'to visit South America' follows as the infinitive: visitar Sudamérica.","'as interesting as' is the equal comparison tan…como: tan interesante como.","Full answer: Te gustaría visitar Sudamérica porque es tan interesante como España."],"cues":["Which pronoun goes with gustaría for the informal singular 'you'?","What follows gustaría to say what you would like to do?","How do you say 'as … as' in Spanish?","Write the complete Spanish sentence, including the reason clause."]},{"title":"Reading comprehension: an impersonal structure and a cultural item","setup":"Read and answer in English: 'En mi pueblo se necesitan más contenedores, sobre todo durante la Semana Santa, cuando hay mucha gente en las calles.' What is needed, and when is the problem worst?","steps":["se necesitan más contenedores: the impersonal se + plural verb agrees with contenedores — 'more bins are needed'.","sobre todo durante la Semana Santa: 'especially during Holy Week', the F/H cultural item.","cuando hay mucha gente en las calles gives the reason: 'when there are a lot of people in the streets'.","Answer: more bins are needed; the problem is worst during Holy Week, when the streets are crowded."],"cues":["What does the se + verb phrase say is needed?","Which cultural item names the time of year?","What reason does the cuando clause give?","What are the two answers in English?"]},{"title":"Reading an appendix entry: a cultural item","setup":"The appendix lists: 'plaza de toros — F/H'. Explain what the code tells you, what kind of item this is, and how you would use it.","steps":["F/H in the Tier column means the item is required at both tiers, so a Foundation candidate must know it as well as a Higher candidate.","It is one of the cultural items the appendix groups with the F/H phrases — known for what it names (the bullring), not for a frequency band.","Use it as a unit: La plaza de toros está en el centro (The bullring is in the centre) — three words learned together with one meaning.","So the full answer: both tiers must know it, it is a cultural item, and in use it names the building as a unit — Hay una plaza de toros en mi ciudad (There is a bullring in my city)."],"cues":["What does the slash in F/H tell you about who must know it?","Is it listed for its frequency, or for what it names?","How would you use it in a sentence?","What is the full answer, with the phrase used in a sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What does the code F/H mean in the Tier column?","a":"Required at both Foundation and Higher tier."},{"q":"Translate: 'Hace dos años empecé a estudiar español.'","a":"Two years ago I started studying Spanish."},{"q":"What is the difference between O and O>?","a":"O is optional but within the top 2000 most frequent words; O> is optional and outside the top 2000 most frequent words."},{"q":"What kind of items does the code C mark?","a":"Cultural and geographical items, such as festivals, foods and places, grouped separately from the frequency-based codes."}]},"room":{"facts":["Tier column: H = Higher tier only; F/H = required at both Foundation and Higher tier.","Selection-principle column: R = Required; O = Optional, within the top 2000 words; O> = Optional, outside the top 2000 words; C = cultural/geographical item.","A Higher tier candidate must know every F/H item as well as every H item — the Higher appendix does not repeat the Foundation list, it only adds to it.","hay que, se puede and se necesita are all impersonal structures (no named subject); tener que and gustaría take a personal subject or indirect object.","tan…como makes an equal comparison ('as … as'); it is different from más…que (more … than) and menos…que (less … than)."],"questions":[{"q":"What does the Tier code H mean for an item in this appendix?","a":"Required only at Higher tier.","sol":"H marks a headword, phrase or irregular form that only a Higher tier candidate is expected to know; it is not on the Foundation tier list at all.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of only two codes in the Tier column.","Which tier or tiers does this particular code apply to?","Name the tier or tiers, in a phrase of three or four words."],"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"What does the Tier code F/H mean for an item in this appendix?","a":"Required at both Foundation and Higher tier.","sol":"F/H marks an item, usually a multi-word phrase or cultural term, that both Foundation and Higher tier candidates are expected to know.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Look at which column this code belongs to.","Does the code point to a single tier, or to more than one?","Name the tier or tiers, in a phrase of three or four words."],"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"An entry is marked 'O>' in the Selection-principle column. What does this tell you about its frequency and its status?","a":"It is optional, and it falls outside the top 2000 most frequent Spanish words.","sol":"O marks an optional item; the > sign narrows this further to say the item is outside (rarer than) the top 2000 most frequent words, unlike plain O, which is optional but still within the top 2000.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Break the code into its two parts: the letter and the symbol after it.","Compare this code with plain O — what does the extra symbol add?","Give two pieces of information: is it required, and how frequent is it?"],"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish, using the formal 'you' (usted): 'You would like to eat tapas in Seville.'","a":"Le gustaría comer tapas en Sevilla.","sol":"Le gustaría comer tapas en Sevilla. The formal 'you' (usted) takes le with gustaría; gustaría is followed directly by the infinitive comer.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Which pronoun stands for usted before a 'would like' verb?","What form does the verb after a 'would like' phrase take?","One sentence: pronoun, verb, infinitive, object, place."],"codes":["Tier H","Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Durante la Semana Santa hay procesiones por las calles, y por la noche se ven fuegos artificiales.'","a":"During Holy Week there are processions through the streets, and at night you see fireworks.","sol":"During Holy Week there are processions through the streets, and at night fireworks are seen (you can see fireworks). Semana Santa (Holy Week) and fuegos artificiales (fireworks) are both F/H cultural items; se ven is a reflexive-passive structure, best rendered as 'you see' or 'are seen'.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Semana Santa and fuegos artificiales are both fixed cultural terms — translate them as set phrases.","se ven does not name who is doing the seeing — how is that usually rendered in English?","Give both halves of the sentence, joined as the original is."],"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'En Nochevieja comemos paella y luego vemos flamenco en la plaza.'","a":"On New Year's Eve we eat paella and then watch flamenco in the square.","sol":"On New Year's Eve we eat paella and then watch flamenco in the square. Nochevieja has an English equivalent (New Year's Eve), while paella and flamenco are cultural items English keeps in Spanish; comemos and vemos are nosotros forms.","hints":["Decide for each cultural word whether English has its own name for it.","Whose actions are comemos and vemos — which person of the verb?","One complete English sentence with two actions joined by and then."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Selection principle"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Mi abuelo dice que hay que visitar la plaza de toros, pero a mí me gustaría ver los fuegos artificiales.' Answer in English: what does the grandfather say people must do, and what would the writer like to do?","a":"Visit the bullring; see the fireworks.","sol":"hay que visitar la plaza de toros — people must visit the bullring (hay que = impersonal obligation); a mí me gustaría ver los fuegos artificiales — I would like to see the fireworks (a mí adds emphasis to me gustaría).","hints":["Find the impersonal obligation phrase and the infinitive after it.","The second half opens with an emphatic pronoun phrase — find the wish that follows it.","Two short English answers, one per half."],"m":2,"d":2,"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'One needs more recycling points in my town.'","a":"Se necesitan más puntos de reciclaje en mi pueblo.","sol":"Se necesitan más puntos de reciclaje en mi pueblo. se necesita(n) is the F/H impersonal structure for 'one needs' / '...is/are needed'; because the subject that follows (puntos de reciclaje) is plural, the verb must also be plural: se necesitan, not se necesita.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["No particular person needs anything here — which impersonal pattern fits?","Is the thing needed singular or plural, and what does that do to the verb?","One sentence: impersonal verb agreeing with a plural noun, then the place."],"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'In Spain you can visit the town hall, but you have to go before five.'","a":"En España se puede visitar el ayuntamiento, pero hay que ir antes de las cinco.","sol":"En España se puede visitar el ayuntamiento, pero hay que ir antes de las cinco. 'you can' with no specific person is the impersonal se puede + infinitive; 'you have to' in the same general sense is hay que + infinitive; antes de las cinco for the time.","hints":["Both 'you' phrases are general, not about one person — which two impersonal structures fit?","Each impersonal structure is followed by an infinitive — pick the two verbs.","Place, impersonal structure + infinitive + object, pero, impersonal structure + infinitive + time."],"m":4,"d":3,"codes":["Tier H","Selection principle"]},{"q":"Write approximately 150 words in Spanish about your town, covering: (1) the problems it has; (2) what should be done about them.","a":"About 150 words covering both bullets, e.g. se necesita más transporte público; hay que mejorar el centro; el ayuntamiento tiene que invertir en un centro para jóvenes.","sol":"Vivo en un pueblo pequeño cerca de Sevilla. Me gusta porque la gente es muy simpática, pero hay problemas. Primero, se necesita más transporte público: solo hay dos autobuses al día, así que los jóvenes tienen que depender de sus padres para ir a la ciudad. Segundo, hay que mejorar el centro, porque muchas tiendas están cerradas y la plaza no es tan bonita como antes. Además, por la noche no se puede hacer nada, porque no hay ni cine ni polideportivo. En mi opinión, el ayuntamiento tiene que invertir en un centro para jóvenes con salas para estudiar, escuchar música y practicar deporte. También creo que hay que organizar más fiestas, como las procesiones de la Semana Santa, porque atraen a turistas y son tan importantes para la economía como las tiendas. Si el pueblo cambia un poco, será tan agradable como cualquier ciudad grande. (146 words: the impersonal structures se necesita, hay que and se puede state the problems and needs; tener que and tan…como carry the solutions and comparisons.)","m":25,"d":3,"hints":["Which structures state a need or an obligation without naming who is responsible?","Pair each problem with an impersonal structure and each solution with an obligation or possibility structure.","Two parts of roughly equal length: the problems, then what should be done, with a closing opinion."],"codes":["Tier H","Selection principle"]},{"q":"Translate into English: 'Después del Bachillerato, mi hermana tiene que trabajar en Sudamérica, pero le gustaría estudiar en España.'","a":"After the Bachillerato (Spanish post-16 qualification), my sister has to work in South America, but she would like to study in Spain.","sol":"Después del Bachillerato — after the Bachillerato (keep the name, gloss it if needed); tiene que trabajar — has to work; en Sudamérica — in South America; le gustaría estudiar en España — she would like to study in Spain (le = she, with gustaría).","hints":["The word after del names a qualification — how does English usually refer to it?","Two verb structures: one obligation, one wish — find the pronoun that shows who has the wish.","One complete English sentence with both halves joined by but."],"m":3,"d":2,"codes":["Tier H"]},{"q":"Translate into Spanish: 'Going to the Sanfermines is as exciting as going to Las Fallas.'","a":"Ir a los Sanfermines es tan emocionante como ir a Las Fallas.","sol":"Ir a los Sanfermines es tan emocionante como ir a Las Fallas. tan…como makes the equal comparison; los Sanfermines and Las Fallas are both C-coded cultural items and keep their capitalised, definite-article forms as proper names of festivals.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Which comparison structure means 'as … as', not 'more … than'?","Check how each festival is written in Spanish in the vocabulary list.","One sentence: infinitive phrase, verb, tan + adjective + como, infinitive phrase."],"codes":["Tier H","Selection principle"]},{"q":"Lee: 'Hay que ahorrar agua, pero en mi ciudad se puede usar el transporte público y la ciudad está más limpia que hace diez años.' Answer in English: what must people do, and how has the city changed?","a":"People must save water; the city is cleaner than ten years ago.","sol":"hay que ahorrar agua — it is necessary to save water; la ciudad está más limpia que hace diez años — the city is cleaner than ten years ago (hace + time = ago). The middle clause (se puede usar el transporte público — public transport can be used) is context and is not required for the two marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the impersonal obligation phrase and what follows it.","Find the comparison with ten years ago.","Answer both parts in English."],"codes":["Tier H"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Translate: 'Le gustaría visitar Sudamérica.'","a":"He/She would like to visit South America."},{"q":"Translate: 'Hay que ahorrar agua.'","a":"You have to / we must save water."},{"q":"Translate: 'Se necesitan más autobuses.'","a":"More buses are needed."},{"q":"What kind of items are marked with the code C?","a":"Cultural and geographical items."}]},"cards":[{"front":"hay que + infinitivo","back":"it is necessary to, you have to (impersonal).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"se puede + infinitivo","back":"one can, you can (impersonal).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"se necesita(n) + sustantivo","back":"one needs, … is/are needed (impersonal).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"tan...como","back":"as … as (equal comparison).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"me/te/le gustaría","back":"I/you(tú)/he-she-you(usted) would like.","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"hace + sustantivo de tiempo","back":"… ago (hace dos años = two years ago).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"España","back":"Spain.","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"Semana Santa","back":"Holy Week.","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"fuegos artificiales","back":"fireworks.","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"plaza de toros","back":"bullring.","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"el Bachillerato","back":"Spanish post-16 qualification (roughly equivalent to A levels).","code":"Tier H","open":false},{"front":"el disfraz","back":"fancy dress, costume.","code":"Selection principle","open":false},{"front":"la corrida","back":"bullfight.","code":"Selection principle","open":false},{"front":"el ayuntamiento","back":"town hall.","code":"Selection principle","open":false},{"front":"Sudamérica","back":"South America.","code":"Tier H","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Higher tier: estructuras F/H más usadas","items":["hay que + infinitivo - it is necessary to, you have to","tener que + infinitivo - to have to","se puede + infinitivo - one can, you can","se necesita(n) + sustantivo - ... is/are needed","me/te/le gustaría - I/you(tú)/he-she-you(usted) would like","hace + sustantivo de tiempo - ... ago","tan...como - as ... as"]},{"kind":"vocab","title":"Vocabulario Higher tier: cultura y geografía (F/H y C)","items":["Semana Santa - Holy Week","fuegos artificiales - fireworks","plaza de toros - bullring","España - Spain","Sudamérica - South America","el Bachillerato - Spanish post-16 qualification","la corrida - bullfight","el Día de Reyes - Epiphany, Three Kings' Day","el Día de Muertos - Day of the Dead","Nochevieja - New Year's Eve","la paella - paella","las tapas - tapas","el disfraz - fancy dress, costume","Las Fallas - Las Fallas festival (Valencia)","la Tomatina - the Tomatina tomato festival","los Sanfermines - the running of the bulls festival (Pamplona)","el flamenco - flamenco","el ayuntamiento - town hall"]},{"kind":"grammar","title":"Gramática: estructuras F/H de este apéndice","items":["hay que + infinitivo (impersonal, sin sujeto). Example: Hay que proteger el medio ambiente. (We must protect the environment.)","tener que + infinitivo (con sujeto personal). Example: Tenemos que reciclar más. (We have to recycle more.)","se puede + infinitivo (impersonal). Example: Se puede reciclar el vidrio aquí. (You can recycle glass here.)","se necesita(n) + sustantivo (impersonal; el verbo concuerda con el sustantivo). Example: Se necesitan más puntos de reciclaje. (More recycling points are needed.)","me/te/le gustaría + infinitivo. Example: Le gustaría visitar Sudamérica. (He/She would like to visit South America.)","hace + sustantivo de tiempo (= '... ago'). Example: Hace dos años empecé a estudiar español. (Two years ago I started studying Spanish.)","tan + adjetivo/adverbio + como (comparación de igualdad). Example: Es tan interesante como España. (It is as interesting as Spain.)"]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-26T17:59:40.543Z","by":"claude-code-hand","models":["claude-fable-5-1","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"hand-written-2026-09-26","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key re-solves correctly and the model 150-word answer matches the Higher Question 3 shape (25 marks, two bullets, ~146 words), so the kit is close to publishable — tidy the hint on se necesitan (it hands over the plural agreement), the duplicated hints on the two code questions, and the repetitive final step of the plaza de toros example."}}}} };
